of data set size

195
00:08:49,570 --> 00:08:52,370
for a wide variety of systems,
different kinds of speech

196
00:08:52,370 --> 00:08:55,010
recognition systems, machine
translation systems, language

197
00:08:55,010 --> 00:08:58,530
models, and they showed
that these things follow

198
00:08:58,530 --> 00:09:02,290
these nice polynomial trends
across a really large number

199
00:09:02,290 --> 00:09:04,330
of domains.

200
00:09:04,330 --> 00:09:05,930
And it was really
ahead of its time,

201
00:09:05,930 --> 00:09:09,970
because 2017 was long before
some of the earlier OpenAI

202
00:09:09,970 --> 00:09:12,890
scaling laws and
so on in the 2020s.

203
00:09:12,890 --> 00:09:15,230
And they talked about stuff
that we talk about today,

204
00:09:15,230 --> 00:09:18,250
like emergence,
where suddenly models

205
00:09:18,250 --> 00:09:19,970
might show certain
kinds of capabilities

206
00:09:19,970 --> 00:09:22,530
because accuracy is a much
more discontinuous measure

207
00:09:22,530 --> 00:09:24,250
than losses.

208
00:09:24,250 --> 00:09:27,490
And really, scaling by compute,
that if we have systems

209
00:09:27,490 --> 00:09:30,650
that scale predictably to
large training data, then

210
00:09:30,650 --> 00:09:32,950
really compute is going
to be really important.

211
00:09:32,950 --> 00:09:36,330
And then finally, since we're
scaling by compute, well,

212
00:09:36,330 --> 00:09:39,390
speed and systems optimization
is going to turn into accuracy.

213
00:09:39,390 --> 00:09:42,070
So in some ways, a lot of
the things that we see today,

214
00:09:42,070 --> 00:09:45,240
like the phenomena of
system scaling and so on,

215
00:09:45,240 --> 00:09:48,440
we could have really known,
or I could have known in 2017

216
00:09:48,440 --> 00:09:52,280
had I read and thought about a
lot of these scaling laws papers

217
00:09:52,280 --> 00:09:54,360
