241
00:11:02,860 --> 00:11:04,693
And they give you
candidate functional forms

242
00:11:04,693 --> 00:11:05,460
of various kinds.

243
00:11:05,460 --> 00:11:06,640
Physicists are also
very good at this

244
00:11:06,640 --> 00:11:07,670
because they thinking
about limits,

245
00:11:07,670 --> 00:11:09,340
like how do the limits behave?

246
00:11:09,340 --> 00:11:12,980
And that gives you different
scaling law functional forms.

247
00:11:12,980 --> 00:11:13,480
Cool.

248
00:11:13,480 --> 00:11:18,640

249
00:11:18,640 --> 00:11:21,560
All right, any other questions?

250
00:11:21,560 --> 00:11:22,840
Good.

251
00:11:22,840 --> 00:11:26,320
So now I'm going to start
talking about more modern neural

252
00:11:26,320 --> 00:11:28,440
language model
scaling, and I'm going

253
00:11:28,440 --> 00:11:31,480
to start with data scaling laws
because to me, they're the most

254
00:11:31,480 --> 00:11:34,320
simple and natural objects.

255
00:11:34,320 --> 00:11:35,960
Talking about data
scaling laws also

256
00:11:35,960 --> 00:11:39,760
allows me to walk you through
very step-by-step on how

257
00:11:39,760 --> 00:11:43,990
we might guess what the
functional form of a scaling law

258
00:11:43,990 --> 00:11:47,150
might be and why the polynomial
form is a natural one.

259
00:11:47,150 --> 00:11:49,670
And then I'm going to start
talking about hyper parameters

260
00:11:49,670 --> 00:11:53,070
and architectures and all sorts
of other more exotic objects

261
00:11:53,070 --> 00:11:55,730
that are going to be harder
to justify from intuition,

262
00:11:55,730 --> 00:12:00,910
but still turn out to have very
nice scaling law behaviors.

263
00:12:00,910 --> 00:12:04,310
One of the things that's
remarkable about this scaling

264
00:12:04,310 --> 00:12:07,950
law style analysis has been that
these power law relationships

265
00:12:07,950 --> 00:12:12,510
seem to hold for a huge
variety of different factors.

266
00:12:12,510 --> 00:12:14,970
So the basic ones that
we see in pre-training,

267
00:12:14,970 --> 00:12:16,762
and those are going to
be the ones that I'm

268
00:12:16,762 --> 00:12:18,230
going to talk about today.

269
00:12:18,230 --> 00:12:21,677
The usual ones that you see
are compute on the x-axis.

270
00:12:21,677 --> 00:12:22,510
This is log compute.

271
00:12:22,510 --> 00:12:25,750
Sorry, on the x-axis, log
of test loss on the y-axis,

272
00:12:25,750 --> 00:12:28,050
or log of the data
set on x-axis,

273
00:12:28,050 --> 00:12:30,550
log of loss on the
y-axis, log of parameters

274
00:12:30,550 --> 00:12:33,610
on x-axis, log of loss,
once again, on the y-axis.

275
00:12:33,610 --> 00:12:36,150
So we can get a variety
of different x-axes

276
00:12:36,150 --> 00:12:39,130
that you might think of as
resources in the log axis.

277
00:12:39,130 --> 00:12:43,100
All of them linearize
log test loss here.

278
00:12:43,100 --> 00:12:47,000
You can see also much
more exotic scaling laws.

279
00:12:47,000 --> 00:12:50,900
You can see scaling
laws that are basically

280
00:12:50,900 --> 00:12:53,280
the equivalent to these
in downstream benchmarks.

281
00:12:53,280 --> 00:12:56,340
In those cases, you expect to
see a sigmoid functional form

282
00:12:56,340 --> 00:12:59,740
as a function of compute
or compute-like quantities.

283
00:12:59,740 --> 00:13:02,340
There's even been many
works recently, more

284
00:13:02,340 --> 00:13:04,700
on the forecasting side,
where people are doing things

285
00:13:04,700 --> 00:13:07,440
like putting dates
as the x-axis.

286
00:13:07,440 --> 00:13:09,140
And strangely enough,
at least for these

287
00:13:09,140 --> 00:13:11,540
plots-- like the upper
envelope of these curves

288
00:13:11,540 --> 00:13:16,220
are often linear in various
forms of capabilities.

289
00:13:16,220 --> 00:13:18,880
There's no reason that
on this bottom side,

290
00:13:18,880 --> 00:13:21,980
the more exotic scaling
laws need to be true.

291
00:13:21,980 --> 00:13:25,540
But it turns out to be the case
that I guess language model

292
00:13:25,540 --> 00:13:28,620
performance is often much more
regular as a function of scale

293
00:13:28,620 --> 00:13:33,860
and resources than it
initially might appear.

294
00:13:33,860 --> 00:13:37,200
So to begin with, I'm going to
start with data scaling laws.

295
00:13:37,200 --> 00:13:39,650
And data scaling laws
are a very simple kind

296
00:13:39,650 --> 00:13:42,530
of univariate relationship that
we're going to think about.

297
00:13:42,530 --> 00:13:45,050
So in this world, we're going
to fix our model training

298
00:13:45,050 --> 00:13:46,010
procedure.

299
00:13:46,010 --> 00:13:48,070
And in general, unless
I say otherwise,

300
00:13:48,070 --> 00:13:49,570
the models are going
to generally be

301
00:13:49,570 --> 00:13:51,530
bigger than the data set size.

302
00:13:51,530 --> 00:13:55,190
And I'm going to increase my
data set size in a regular way,

303
00:13:55,190 --> 00:13:57,390
and I'm going to see
how the error reduces.

304
00:13:57,390 --> 00:13:59,690
And what do we expect
out of this relationship?

305
00:13:59,690 --> 00:14:02,250
In general, it's going
to be monotone-ish

306
00:14:02,250 --> 00:14:03,990
if we tune our
hyperparameters well.

307
00:14:03,990 --> 00:14:05,970
Because we have
more data, more data

308
00:14:05,970 --> 00:14:08,550
should help us do
better at our task.

309
00:14:08,550 --> 00:14:10,050
The error should decrease.

310
00:14:10,050 --> 00:14:12,570
And if you're doing something
like classification, or even

311
00:14:12,570 --> 00:14:14,810
for next token prediction,
you should really

312
00:14:14,810 --> 00:14:18,490
go from random guessing to
some of entropy-like object

313
00:14:18,490 --> 00:14:21,690
where you can't do any better
than the irreducible error

314
00:14:21,690 --> 00:14:22,350
of your task.

315
00:14:22,350 --> 00:14:23,350
That's the noise floor.

316
00:14:23,350 --> 00:14:26,970
So we're going to have some
sigmoidal-like object over here.

317
00:14:26,970 --> 00:14:31,290
So this is generally what
we expect out of our system.

318
00:14:31,290 --> 00:14:34,630
So we're going to start with
an empirical observation,

319
00:14:34,630 --> 00:14:38,510
and should make
this on your own.

320
00:14:38,510 --> 00:14:40,370
You'll make something
that looks roughly

321
00:14:40,370 --> 00:14:43,610
like this in parts
of assignment 3,

322
00:14:43,610 --> 00:14:46,410
which is if we just
have a very big model,

323
00:14:46,410 --> 00:14:48,650
much bigger than
our data set, and we

324
00:14:48,650 --> 00:14:51,530
increase the data set size, and
we put that on the log axis,

325
00:14:51,530 --> 00:14:54,230
and on the y-axis, we
have log test loss.

326
00:14:54,230 --> 00:14:59,050
Once again, you will find that
the two points that you plot

327
00:14:59,050 --> 00:15:01,790
will form a very
clear linear trend.

328
00:15:01,790 --> 00:15:04,890
I've taken this
from Kaplan in 2020.

329
00:15:04,890 --> 00:15:06,870
I'm going to refer
repeatedly to this paper.

330
00:15:06,870 --> 00:15:09,970
This one's OpenAI's "Neural
Scaling Laws" paper.

331
00:15:09,970 --> 00:15:11,890
They have a lot of
lovely ablations

332
00:15:11,890 --> 00:15:13,930
and a lot of lovely data.

333
00:15:13,930 --> 00:15:16,610
Now, this relationship,
where I've

334
00:15:16,610 --> 00:15:18,910
plotted a line on
a log-log plot,

335
00:15:18,910 --> 00:15:23,090
this is a scale-free or
power law relationship.

336
00:15:23,090 --> 00:15:24,110
And what does that mean?

337
00:15:24,110 --> 00:15:26,258
If I have a line
on a log-log plot?

338
00:15:26,258 --> 00:15:28,550
Many of you-- to many of you,
this is probably obvious.

339
00:15:28,550 --> 00:15:30,210
But just to really
emphasize this,

340
00:15:30,210 --> 00:15:32,130
if something is linear
in a log-log plot, what

341
00:15:32,130 --> 00:15:33,050
does that mean?

342
00:15:33,050 --> 00:15:35,530
Well, it means that
the error that I have

343
00:15:35,530 --> 00:15:37,280
is decaying polynomially.

344
00:15:37,280 --> 00:15:39,400
And also, it usually means
that I'm very far away

345
00:15:39,400 --> 00:15:40,340
from my asymptote.

346
00:15:40,340 --> 00:15:41,940
Because once I
approach my asymptote,

347
00:15:41,940 --> 00:15:45,280
I'm going to taper off
rather than have a line.

348
00:15:45,280 --> 00:15:48,320
So let's go through an
example just to show you

349
00:15:48,320 --> 00:15:51,880
how scaling law arises
very naturally on a very

350
00:15:51,880 --> 00:15:55,120
simple estimation problem.

351
00:15:55,120 --> 00:15:57,420
So forget language
modeling for the moment.

352
00:15:57,420 --> 00:15:59,140
This was a language
modeling class,

353
00:15:59,140 --> 00:16:01,200
but now it is a statistics
class for the next one

354
00:16:01,200 --> 00:16:03,280
slide or actually two slides.

355
00:16:03,280 --> 00:16:06,080
No, maybe three slides.

356
00:16:06,080 --> 00:16:07,720
So in this case,
what I want to do

357
00:16:07,720 --> 00:16:10,535
is I want to estimate the
mean from a bunch of data.

358
00:16:10,535 --> 00:16:12,660
And so I have an input,
it's drawn from a Gaussian.

359
00:16:12,660 --> 00:16:14,420
And I'm going to estimate
the mean of this object.

360
00:16:14,420 --> 00:16:16,240
I just write down
the empirical mean.

361
00:16:16,240 --> 00:16:18,220
And then, you can write
down what the error is.

362
00:16:18,220 --> 00:16:19,428
So what's the expected error?

363
00:16:19,428 --> 00:16:21,415
Well, each of these
guys is Gaussian,

364
00:16:21,415 --> 00:16:24,040
and that the mu hat is Gaussian,
so I can write down the error.

365
00:16:24,040 --> 00:16:26,480
That's going to be
sigma squared over n.

366
00:16:26,480 --> 00:16:30,900
So well, if you look at
this, is a scaling law.

367
00:16:30,900 --> 00:16:35,030
I've written down the error,
and the error, if I log that,

368
00:16:35,030 --> 00:16:40,590
is a function of log n,
log n being the log x-axis.

369
00:16:40,590 --> 00:16:42,710
So in general, if
you can come up

370
00:16:42,710 --> 00:16:44,990
with anything that
is essentially

371
00:16:44,990 --> 00:16:47,950
1 over n to the alpha
plus some constant,

372
00:16:47,950 --> 00:16:50,090
when you plot that
on a log-log plot,

373
00:16:50,090 --> 00:16:51,510
subtracting out
the constant term,

374
00:16:51,510 --> 00:16:53,295
you're going to
get a scaling log.

375
00:16:53,295 --> 00:16:55,670
So anything of this form,
where you have polynomial decay

376
00:16:55,670 --> 00:16:59,250
and errors, is going to end
up giving you a scaling law.

377
00:16:59,250 --> 00:17:02,230
And of course, in parametric
estimation like mean estimation

378
00:17:02,230 --> 00:17:05,329
or regression, you of course,
expect a polynomial rate.

379
00:17:05,329 --> 00:17:08,829
You don't really
expect anything else.

380
00:17:08,829 --> 00:17:11,589
And if you've taken your
stats estimation courses,

381
00:17:11,589 --> 00:17:14,030
you know that essentially
all the classical

382
00:17:14,030 --> 00:17:17,349
models you are expected to
have a 1 over n scaling.

383
00:17:17,349 --> 00:17:19,990
If you fit a regression or
so on, you might get D over n

384
00:17:19,990 --> 00:17:21,710
or whatever else.

385
00:17:21,710 --> 00:17:25,470
And so if this is true, and this
is the origins of scaling laws,

386
00:17:25,470 --> 00:17:27,710
if we plot our lines,
we should expect

387
00:17:27,710 --> 00:17:30,830
to see roughly a
slope of minus 1

388
00:17:30,830 --> 00:17:32,740
because that's the
exponent on the x.

389
00:17:32,740 --> 00:17:34,780
Y equals minus x plus C.

390
00:17:34,780 --> 00:17:36,760
If we fit our
neural scaling laws,

391
00:17:36,760 --> 00:17:39,740
we will find that our
exponents are quite different.

392
00:17:39,740 --> 00:17:43,300
These exponents-- I've
taken from these two

393
00:17:43,300 --> 00:17:45,780
on the left from Hestenes,
I've taken the one on the right

394
00:17:45,780 --> 00:17:46,860
from Kaplan.

395
00:17:46,860 --> 00:17:51,600
They're about -0.1, -0.3,
-0.1, roughly speaking.

396
00:17:51,600 --> 00:17:53,760
And so what does that mean?

397
00:17:53,760 --> 00:17:56,740
That means this is much
slower than estimating a mean

398
00:17:56,740 --> 00:17:58,880
or estimating a
linear regression.

399
00:17:58,880 --> 00:18:01,540
So this is much, much slower
convergence rate, still

400
00:18:01,540 --> 00:18:03,740
polynomial, but much slower.

401
00:18:03,740 --> 00:18:05,760
Now this is interesting.

402
00:18:05,760 --> 00:18:07,820
It's saying that we're
getting these kind

403
00:18:07,820 --> 00:18:11,320
of nice polynomial rates, but
the exponents are not so great.

404
00:18:11,320 --> 00:18:12,780
They're -0.1.

405
00:18:12,780 --> 00:18:15,460
Where might you get an
estimation rate like this?

406
00:18:15,460 --> 00:18:19,680
Well, you would get this if your
model was more nonparametric.

407
00:18:19,680 --> 00:18:21,460
I promise this will
be the last statistics

408
00:18:21,460 --> 00:18:23,460
slide of this lecture.

409
00:18:23,460 --> 00:18:25,100
Let's say that I
want to estimate now,

410
00:18:25,100 --> 00:18:28,040
instead of a mean, an
arbitrary smooth function.

411
00:18:28,040 --> 00:18:31,780
So one thing I could do is, if
I'm doing a regression problem,

412
00:18:31,780 --> 00:18:34,220
I have my inputs, x1 through xn.

413
00:18:34,220 --> 00:18:35,777
Let's say they're
in the unit box.

414
00:18:35,777 --> 00:18:37,360
I have a little bit
of Gaussian noise,

415
00:18:37,360 --> 00:18:40,540
and I have an unknown function
f that I would like to estimate.

416
00:18:40,540 --> 00:18:44,320
A trivial estimator for
this is I can take my space,

417
00:18:44,320 --> 00:18:47,100
I can cut it up into
little 2D boxes of length n

418
00:18:47,100 --> 00:18:49,460
to the negative 1 to the fourth.

419
00:18:49,460 --> 00:18:51,160
And then what's our
estimation error?

420
00:18:51,160 --> 00:18:53,000
I'll have square root n boxes.

421
00:18:53,000 --> 00:18:55,557
Each box is going to get
square root n samples.

422
00:18:55,557 --> 00:18:57,140
And the error is
going to be roughly 1

423
00:18:57,140 --> 00:18:58,840
over square root
of n or something.

424
00:18:58,840 --> 00:19:02,400
And in general, if have
a D-dimensional function

425
00:19:02,400 --> 00:19:06,180
I would like to estimate in this
very nonparametric, arbitrary

426
00:19:06,180 --> 00:19:09,340
way, my error is going
to be roughly equal to n

427
00:19:09,340 --> 00:19:11,040
to the negative 1 over d.

428
00:19:11,040 --> 00:19:13,180
So my scaling is going
to be y equals negative 1

429
00:19:13,180 --> 00:19:14,940
over D times x.

430
00:19:14,940 --> 00:19:17,860
And so now these kinds more
flexible functions, functions

431
00:19:17,860 --> 00:19:20,260
that are more flexible
than the linear class,

432
00:19:20,260 --> 00:19:23,940
now have rates in their scaling
laws that are not just 1,

433
00:19:23,940 --> 00:19:25,080
they're 1 over D.

434
00:19:25,080 --> 00:19:28,380
And so one mental model
that you could have

435
00:19:28,380 --> 00:19:31,450
is that the neural networks I
was showing you in these slides

436
00:19:31,450 --> 00:19:34,610
are behaving kind of
non-parametric regressors in 10

437
00:19:34,610 --> 00:19:36,730
dimensions, like a nearest
neighbor or other kinds

438
00:19:36,730 --> 00:19:38,750
of estimators in 10 dimensions.

439
00:19:38,750 --> 00:19:41,230
That's roughly the rate at
which they learn from data.

440
00:19:41,230 --> 00:19:42,605
That's kind of a
cool thing to be

441
00:19:42,605 --> 00:19:46,850
able to know just by looking
at some of these scaling laws.

442
00:19:46,850 --> 00:19:49,690
Some people have argued
this more strongly.

443
00:19:49,690 --> 00:19:53,010
Bari et al. and some other
scaling law theory folks

444
00:19:53,010 --> 00:19:56,090
have argued that the scaling
law exponents are actually

445
00:19:56,090 --> 00:19:58,530
literally telling us
that the neural network

446
00:19:58,530 --> 00:20:00,730
is behaving like a
non-parametric smoother,

447
00:20:00,730 --> 00:20:02,568
and so on and so forth.

448
00:20:02,568 --> 00:20:04,110
It's an interesting
set of arguments,

449
00:20:04,110 --> 00:20:07,330
and you can by that intuition
to whatever extent you'd like.

450
00:20:07,330 --> 00:20:10,490
I don't quite how much I
truly, truly buy this argument.

451
00:20:10,490 --> 00:20:13,350
There's some of the evidence
that might be a little sketchy.

452
00:20:13,350 --> 00:20:16,297
It relies on estimators
of intrinsic dimension,

453
00:20:16,297 --> 00:20:18,130
but it's an interesting
thing to think about

454
00:20:18,130 --> 00:20:20,570
that somehow these
exponents are telling us

455
00:20:20,570 --> 00:20:24,490
how fast are these neural
networks really learning.

456
00:20:24,490 --> 00:20:27,830
OK, so actually, I'll
pause for the moment here.

457
00:20:27,830 --> 00:20:30,360
That's really the end of the
really basic data scaling

458
00:20:30,360 --> 00:20:33,200
law of one distribution,
and you sample from it,

459
00:20:33,200 --> 00:20:34,320
and you learn from it.

460
00:20:34,320 --> 00:20:37,700
Is that all clear, like
the examples and so on?

461
00:20:37,700 --> 00:20:38,200
Good.

462
00:20:38,200 --> 00:20:39,620
Oh yes, question.

463
00:20:39,620 --> 00:20:42,280
When you say that the
model is always larger

464
00:20:42,280 --> 00:20:45,510
than data set size, do you mean
one-to-one number of parameters

465
00:20:45,510 --> 00:20:47,652
to number of tokens [INAUDIBLE]?

466
00:20:47,652 --> 00:20:48,860
Yeah, that's a good question.

467
00:20:48,860 --> 00:20:49,760
So the question
was something like,

468
00:20:49,760 --> 00:20:51,400
What do I mean when I
say the model is larger

469
00:20:51,400 --> 00:20:52,580
than the data set size?

470
00:20:52,580 --> 00:20:58,250
What I mean is when the model
gets small relative to the data,

471
00:20:58,250 --> 00:21:00,900
like you have way more data
than you have model size,

472
00:21:00,900 --> 00:21:03,000
you enter this
irreducible error regime.

473
00:21:03,000 --> 00:21:05,400
You basically fitted
the data as well

474
00:21:05,400 --> 00:21:07,120
as you can for your
model class, and you

475
00:21:07,120 --> 00:21:10,280
can't do any better no
matter how much data you add.

476
00:21:10,280 --> 00:21:11,115
So we want to--

477
00:21:11,115 --> 00:21:12,740
when we're thinking
about scaling laws,

478
00:21:12,740 --> 00:21:15,840
we usually want to think about
this power law regime, which

479
00:21:15,840 --> 00:21:17,060
is the simpler case.

480
00:21:17,060 --> 00:21:19,440
Either that or you
explicitly fit the asymptote

481
00:21:19,440 --> 00:21:20,618
and then you correct for it.

482
00:21:20,618 --> 00:21:21,660
Those are the two things.

483
00:21:21,660 --> 00:21:24,020
So when I say the model
is larger than the data,

484
00:21:24,020 --> 00:21:27,095
it can be basically any amount
that is larger than the data,

485
00:21:27,095 --> 00:21:28,470
as long as it's
in this power law

486
00:21:28,470 --> 00:21:31,710
regime, usually like 10 times
bigger than the data, what

487
00:21:31,710 --> 00:21:33,470
have you.

488
00:21:33,470 --> 00:21:34,310
Cool.

489
00:21:34,310 --> 00:21:35,030
Good.

490
00:21:35,030 --> 00:21:39,250
OK, now we are back to
other data scaling laws.

491
00:21:39,250 --> 00:21:42,790
So data scaling
laws by themselves

492
00:21:42,790 --> 00:21:45,610
are not quite as useful
as you'd like them to be.

493
00:21:45,610 --> 00:21:48,450
They just tell you how
fast does my model learn.

494
00:21:48,450 --> 00:21:51,250
And that's kind of
useful for forecasting.

495
00:21:51,250 --> 00:21:53,870
It's not useful
for very much else.

496
00:21:53,870 --> 00:21:55,310
If you want to do
engineering, you

497
00:21:55,310 --> 00:21:57,190
are probably interested
in other questions,

498
00:21:57,190 --> 00:21:59,730
like what is my
optimal data mixture?

499
00:21:59,730 --> 00:22:02,190
What is the best way for
me to pick my training

500
00:22:02,190 --> 00:22:03,330
data for my model?

501
00:22:03,330 --> 00:22:05,550
Very useful
engineering question.

502
00:22:05,550 --> 00:22:07,790
Should I repeat my
data, or should I

503
00:22:07,790 --> 00:22:12,150
just not repeat my data, save
the compute for something else?

504
00:22:12,150 --> 00:22:15,350
Or maybe I should repeat
some high-quality stuff

505
00:22:15,350 --> 00:22:18,050
and not repeat the
low-quality stuff.

506
00:22:18,050 --> 00:22:20,310
I could do various other
things with repetition.

507
00:22:20,310 --> 00:22:22,430
There's a lot of different
engineering decisions

508
00:22:22,430 --> 00:22:23,450
I can make with data.

509
00:22:23,450 --> 00:22:28,420
So how can scaling laws help us
make some of those decisions?

510
00:22:28,420 --> 00:22:30,420
And I think one of the
things that's interesting

511
00:22:30,420 --> 00:22:33,820
is if we think a little
bit about how classical

512
00:22:33,820 --> 00:22:35,500
models behave.

513
00:22:35,500 --> 00:22:38,340
Data scaling laws, if we
think about their origins

514
00:22:38,340 --> 00:22:41,420
as essentially empirical
versions of generalization

515
00:22:41,420 --> 00:22:45,700
bounds, they tell us, well, data
set composition for many models

516
00:22:45,700 --> 00:22:48,460
really affect the offset
of these scaling laws,

517
00:22:48,460 --> 00:22:51,498
but their slopes are actually
determined by the model class.

518
00:22:51,498 --> 00:22:54,040
They're not really determined
by the distribution themselves.

519
00:22:54,040 --> 00:22:55,540
So the slopes might
remain the same,

520
00:22:55,540 --> 00:22:57,333
and only the intercepts
might change.

521
00:22:57,333 --> 00:23:00,000
And the intercept might actually
be really interestingly shaped.

522
00:23:00,000 --> 00:23:03,780
So this is a case of a toy
model with a linear regression

523
00:23:03,780 --> 00:23:05,960
with two different things
you can sample from.

524
00:23:05,960 --> 00:23:08,420
If you get only
one kind of data,

525
00:23:08,420 --> 00:23:10,280
it's the errors are
high no matter what.

526
00:23:10,280 --> 00:23:12,040
You actually want a
mixture of both right.

527
00:23:12,040 --> 00:23:15,640
So if you write down how
this intercept behaves,

528
00:23:15,640 --> 00:23:18,580
you actually start to get
some very interesting insights

529
00:23:18,580 --> 00:23:22,020
about how having more data
diversity is very helpful.

530
00:23:22,020 --> 00:23:25,140
But what does this
practically mean?

531
00:23:25,140 --> 00:23:27,660
Well, practically
speaking, what this means

532
00:23:27,660 --> 00:23:31,400
is you can actually fit data
scaling laws on mixtures,

533
00:23:31,400 --> 00:23:34,080
and that can help
you do some data--

534
00:23:34,080 --> 00:23:37,122
sorry, pre-training,
data optimization.

535
00:23:37,122 --> 00:23:38,080
So what does this mean?

536
00:23:38,080 --> 00:23:41,160
Let's say you have two data
sources for simplicity.

537
00:23:41,160 --> 00:23:43,480
You have news, and
you have Wikipedia.

538
00:23:43,480 --> 00:23:46,580
And how much news to use and
how much Wikipedia to use.

539
00:23:46,580 --> 00:23:48,000
How can you determine that?

540
00:23:48,000 --> 00:23:50,820
Well, you can train
really small models

541
00:23:50,820 --> 00:23:52,795
on a really small
amount of data.

542
00:23:52,795 --> 00:23:54,420
And then you can
basically train or you

543
00:23:54,420 --> 00:23:58,500
can fit a function of how
different mixture levels affect

544
00:23:58,500 --> 00:23:59,640
your performance.

545
00:23:59,640 --> 00:24:03,400
You can do this while you scale
up your models little by little.

546
00:24:03,400 --> 00:24:05,083
And you can see how
the trends change.

547
00:24:05,083 --> 00:24:07,500
And you can try to extrapolate
that out the exact same way

548
00:24:07,500 --> 00:24:09,860
as you would extrapolate
out a data scaling law.

549
00:24:09,860 --> 00:24:11,900
And then you can ask
which one will be optimal

550
00:24:11,900 --> 00:24:14,460
if I keep scaling it out
to my full training run.

551
00:24:14,460 --> 00:24:18,740
So this is a very simple
idea of essentially fitting

552
00:24:18,740 --> 00:24:21,530
a functional form at a
small amount of compute,

553
00:24:21,530 --> 00:24:24,610
finding the minimum, and
then scaling that guy out.

554
00:24:24,610 --> 00:24:28,530
So this is a data
mixing loss paper.

555
00:24:28,530 --> 00:24:30,050
Unfortunately, I
think if you talk

556
00:24:30,050 --> 00:24:32,810
to anyone who's done a lot
of these data mixture work,

557
00:24:32,810 --> 00:24:35,370
they will tell you
reality is a lot more

558
00:24:35,370 --> 00:24:38,730
noisy than this ideal
world would suggest.

559
00:24:38,730 --> 00:24:42,450
And really, what has, as far as
I know, happened in many cases

560
00:24:42,450 --> 00:24:45,430
is you end up training
a bunch of small models.

561
00:24:45,430 --> 00:24:48,010
You pick the best data
mix from the small model,

562
00:24:48,010 --> 00:24:51,250
and you just scale that guy
up, no scaling law required.

563
00:24:51,250 --> 00:24:53,930
And data decide if you're
interested in this kind of thing

564
00:24:53,930 --> 00:24:58,650
is a nice, large-scale empirical
study of these kinds of data

565
00:24:58,650 --> 00:24:59,930
mixture things.

566
00:24:59,930 --> 00:25:01,710
And what they
found was actually,

567
00:25:01,710 --> 00:25:03,890
if you don't fit a scaling
law, and instead you

568
00:25:03,890 --> 00:25:06,350
just pick the best data
mix at the small scale,

569
00:25:06,350 --> 00:25:07,810
that works well.

570
00:25:07,810 --> 00:25:10,290
For what it's worth, that's
consistent with the argument

571
00:25:10,290 --> 00:25:13,250
that the intercepts differ,
but the slopes don't change.

572
00:25:13,250 --> 00:25:15,150
Because if the
slopes don't change,

573
00:25:15,150 --> 00:25:17,850
the best mixture at small
scale is also the best mixture

574
00:25:17,850 --> 00:25:20,080
at large scale.

575
00:25:20,080 --> 00:25:23,760
OK, another really
interesting set

576
00:25:23,760 --> 00:25:27,800
of questions that I'll also
mention in the context of data

577
00:25:27,800 --> 00:25:29,440
is repetition.

578
00:25:29,440 --> 00:25:33,820
I think it's increasingly the
case that compute is growing,

579
00:25:33,820 --> 00:25:35,932
the amount of data that
we have is not growing.

580
00:25:35,932 --> 00:25:37,640
And so there's a lot
of people interested

581
00:25:37,640 --> 00:25:40,160
in questions of what
happens when you repeat

582
00:25:40,160 --> 00:25:42,600
data more and more times.

583
00:25:42,600 --> 00:25:45,720
And there was a nice study a few
years back called "Scaling Data-

584
00:25:45,720 --> 00:25:48,000
Constrained Language Models,"
where they roughly show

585
00:25:48,000 --> 00:25:51,300
something like up to four epochs
with standard training recipes,

586
00:25:51,300 --> 00:25:53,280
you just don't get hurt at all.

587
00:25:53,280 --> 00:25:56,040
But if you go past that
point, then actually

588
00:25:56,040 --> 00:25:58,840
your realized scaling
law, this dark curve

589
00:25:58,840 --> 00:26:01,543
is much worse than the
projected scaling law

590
00:26:01,543 --> 00:26:02,460
if you had fresh data.

591
00:26:02,460 --> 00:26:04,217
That's the dashed
line over here.

592
00:26:04,217 --> 00:26:06,800
And you can also, once again,
write down a modified functional

593
00:26:06,800 --> 00:26:11,120
form that's generally predictive
of the behavior of this scaling

594
00:26:11,120 --> 00:26:14,440
law under repetition.

595
00:26:14,440 --> 00:26:17,140
And we can take this idea
even more to the extreme.

596
00:26:17,140 --> 00:26:19,990
This was kind of
recent work with Percy

597
00:26:19,990 --> 00:26:21,530
in one of our
co-advise students,

598
00:26:21,530 --> 00:26:23,030
where we were asking,
like, OK, what

599
00:26:23,030 --> 00:26:25,030
happens if you take
this idea to the extreme

600
00:26:25,030 --> 00:26:27,570
and you just consider
infinite amounts of compute?

601
00:26:27,570 --> 00:26:29,850
So you're allowed to epoch
as many times as you want,

602
00:26:29,850 --> 00:26:31,332
like an infinite
amount of times.

603
00:26:31,332 --> 00:26:33,790
What is the best thing that
you can get out of this system?

604
00:26:33,790 --> 00:26:35,350
Well, it turns
out you can't just

605
00:26:35,350 --> 00:26:37,422
keep repeating
passes over the data.

606
00:26:37,422 --> 00:26:39,130
You can't keep making
your models bigger.

607
00:26:39,130 --> 00:26:40,590
Those have diminishing returns.

608
00:26:40,590 --> 00:26:42,570
And so you end up
reaching for other things,

609
00:26:42,570 --> 00:26:45,342
like ensembling your models to
try to squeeze more and more

610
00:26:45,342 --> 00:26:46,050
out of your data.

611
00:26:46,050 --> 00:26:48,258
But really, I think the one
thing that I'll point out

612
00:26:48,258 --> 00:26:50,350
that's really interesting
is your standard data

613
00:26:50,350 --> 00:26:51,690
scaling laws here in the red.

614
00:26:51,690 --> 00:26:55,110
It's a very nice, predictable,
improvement in performance

615
00:26:55,110 --> 00:26:56,810
as you increase
the amount of data.

616
00:26:56,810 --> 00:26:59,830
We do all sorts of interventions
like regularizing and adding

617
00:26:59,830 --> 00:27:00,950
ensembles.

618
00:27:00,950 --> 00:27:02,850
You get improvements
in performance,

619
00:27:02,850 --> 00:27:05,690
but the slopes look actually
surprisingly similar.

620
00:27:05,690 --> 00:27:08,117
This is a lesson
that you'll learn

621
00:27:08,117 --> 00:27:10,450
once you start fitting your
own scaling laws, that very,

622
00:27:10,450 --> 00:27:13,410
very often your
slopes don't change.

623
00:27:13,410 --> 00:27:15,150
Often, the interventions
that you do just

624
00:27:15,150 --> 00:27:19,710
change the intercept
of the scaling laws.

625
00:27:19,710 --> 00:27:22,070
One last thing
that I'll mention,

626
00:27:22,070 --> 00:27:24,550
because I like this paper
in the context of scaling

627
00:27:24,550 --> 00:27:27,670
laws and scaling in
general, is that a lot

628
00:27:27,670 --> 00:27:30,070
of the phenomena we
care about in data

629
00:27:30,070 --> 00:27:31,950
is actually very
scale-dependent.

630
00:27:31,950 --> 00:27:34,790
And one example of
this is data filtering.

631
00:27:34,790 --> 00:27:37,870
So if you or I tomorrow
decide to filter some data

632
00:27:37,870 --> 00:27:39,870
for the models we're
training, we would probably

633
00:27:39,870 --> 00:27:41,090
filter very aggressively.

634
00:27:41,090 --> 00:27:43,590
We would only keep the
highest quality stuff

635
00:27:43,590 --> 00:27:45,770
because both you and I don't
have very much compute.

636
00:27:45,770 --> 00:27:48,050
So we can't train on
all the internet anyway.

637
00:27:48,050 --> 00:27:52,190
We might as well filter down
to the most high-quality stuff.

638
00:27:52,190 --> 00:27:54,635
On the other hand, if you
have a lot of compute,

639
00:27:54,635 --> 00:27:56,510
then you want to train
on more and more stuff

640
00:27:56,510 --> 00:27:58,470
because you're going
to not want to repeat

641
00:27:58,470 --> 00:28:00,150
on this very high-quality data.

642
00:28:00,150 --> 00:28:01,690
So as you get more
and more compute,

643
00:28:01,690 --> 00:28:03,958
your filter has become
looser and looser,

644
00:28:03,958 --> 00:28:05,750
and potentially you
start training on stuff

645
00:28:05,750 --> 00:28:07,830
that's lower and lower quality.

646
00:28:07,830 --> 00:28:09,390
So a lot of the
things that we often

647
00:28:09,390 --> 00:28:11,710
think about statically,
things like data

648
00:28:11,710 --> 00:28:13,578
filtering or the
quality of data,

649
00:28:13,578 --> 00:28:14,870
are actually much more dynamic.

650
00:28:14,870 --> 00:28:17,305
As you increase scale,
you have to think about

651
00:28:17,305 --> 00:28:19,180
where am I going to get
the rest of the data.

652
00:28:19,180 --> 00:28:22,000
The filters can't stay fixed
as a function of the data,

653
00:28:22,000 --> 00:28:23,580
and certainly the
optimal filters

654
00:28:23,580 --> 00:28:25,960
turn out to not be fixed
at a function of scale.

655
00:28:25,960 --> 00:28:28,660

656
00:28:28,660 --> 00:28:31,838
OK, so that's it for
data scaling laws.

657
00:28:31,838 --> 00:28:33,380
This is the part
that I think is most

658
00:28:33,380 --> 00:28:34,640
straightforward to understand.

659
00:28:34,640 --> 00:28:37,720
Even as a
stats/ML-minded person,

660
00:28:37,720 --> 00:28:40,880
this stuff is all very simple
and straightforward to me.

661
00:28:40,880 --> 00:28:42,820
You increase data,
performance gets better

662
00:28:42,820 --> 00:28:44,060
in a very predictable way.

663
00:28:44,060 --> 00:28:45,720
I think this is uncontroversial.

664
00:28:45,720 --> 00:28:47,980
The fact that it does
so polynomially for such

665
00:28:47,980 --> 00:28:52,220
a big model is still cool,
but nothing really crazy.

666
00:28:52,220 --> 00:28:55,020
So hopefully this is
reasonable to all of you

667
00:28:55,020 --> 00:28:57,860
that there's so much
predictability when you

668
00:28:57,860 --> 00:29:00,900
increase the amount of data.

669
00:29:00,900 --> 00:29:02,940
Oh, yes.

670
00:29:02,940 --> 00:29:05,140
So on this slide
for the pre-training

671
00:29:05,140 --> 00:29:10,620
under infinite compute,
so that graph on the right

672
00:29:10,620 --> 00:29:16,450
is linear in both the
loss and data set size,

673
00:29:16,450 --> 00:29:20,150
whereas the previous example
I gave was log-log scale.

674
00:29:20,150 --> 00:29:22,450
And I was wondering why
it's linear in both.

675
00:29:22,450 --> 00:29:25,750
Yeah, so this is a bad axis.

676
00:29:25,750 --> 00:29:28,890
Herman and I just talked
about bad axis like this.

677
00:29:28,890 --> 00:29:30,390
This is actually doubling.

678
00:29:30,390 --> 00:29:32,390
So it looks linear, but
it's actually log scale.

679
00:29:32,390 --> 00:29:32,890
[LAUGHS]

680
00:29:32,890 --> 00:29:33,730
I fixed it now.

681
00:29:33,730 --> 00:29:35,210
You fixed it now.

682
00:29:35,210 --> 00:29:38,310
And the y-axis here,
it doesn't look logged,

683
00:29:38,310 --> 00:29:39,723
but the range of
this is so small

684
00:29:39,723 --> 00:29:41,390
that linear logs are
basically the same.

685
00:29:41,390 --> 00:29:43,510
If you look at the fitted
functional forms here,

686
00:29:43,510 --> 00:29:44,750
these are still polynomials.

687
00:29:44,750 --> 00:29:46,542
So we're fitting the
same functional forms,

688
00:29:46,542 --> 00:29:48,773
and those are the ones
that are plotted here.

689
00:29:48,773 --> 00:29:49,690
This is another point.

690
00:29:49,690 --> 00:29:52,850
I'll get to it at the
end of the lecture.

691
00:29:52,850 --> 00:29:55,610
If you only have a tiny
slice of a compute range,

692
00:29:55,610 --> 00:29:57,170
it's very, very
difficult to tell

693
00:29:57,170 --> 00:29:58,770
if something is
scaling polynomially

694
00:29:58,770 --> 00:30:00,550
or if something's
scaling exponentially.

695
00:30:00,550 --> 00:30:02,510
Because Taylor
approximations are a thing.

696
00:30:02,510 --> 00:30:05,070
Everything looks linear
if you zoom in enough.

697
00:30:05,070 --> 00:30:07,753
And so whether
this is polynomial

698
00:30:07,753 --> 00:30:09,170
or some other
functional form, you

699
00:30:09,170 --> 00:30:13,500
want to be always a little
bit careful and skeptical.

700
00:30:13,500 --> 00:30:14,000
Good.

701
00:30:14,000 --> 00:30:16,120
OK.

702
00:30:16,120 --> 00:30:19,560
All right, so that was
scaling laws for data,

703
00:30:19,560 --> 00:30:21,080
in many ways straightforward.

704
00:30:21,080 --> 00:30:24,840
Now I want to talk about
more exotic forms of scaling

705
00:30:24,840 --> 00:30:29,217
and scaling laws for model
engineering of various kinds.

706
00:30:29,217 --> 00:30:30,800
So what we're going
to do now is we're

707
00:30:30,800 --> 00:30:34,480
going to try to design
large language models.

708
00:30:34,480 --> 00:30:37,745
Maybe you're a radical, and
you believe that maybe LSTMs

709
00:30:37,745 --> 00:30:38,370
are the future.

710
00:30:38,370 --> 00:30:41,480
Like, why can't I use LSTM
instead of a transformer?

711
00:30:41,480 --> 00:30:44,380
I want to blow my
B200 run on my LSTMs.

712
00:30:44,380 --> 00:30:46,600
Or maybe you're a
different kind of radical

713
00:30:46,600 --> 00:30:48,020
and you want to train on SGD.

714
00:30:48,020 --> 00:30:50,600
Like, why can't I train on SGD?

715
00:30:50,600 --> 00:30:51,780
You might also wonder.

716
00:30:51,780 --> 00:30:54,300
If you have a limited
amount of resources,

717
00:30:54,300 --> 00:30:56,760
you have different
ways to spend it.

718
00:30:56,760 --> 00:30:58,417
Maybe you should
train models longer,

719
00:30:58,417 --> 00:31:00,000
maybe you train
bigger models, maybe I

720
00:31:00,000 --> 00:31:01,380
should go collect more data.

721
00:31:01,380 --> 00:31:03,640
There's all sorts of
trade-offs always at play,

722
00:31:03,640 --> 00:31:06,720
and scaling laws give us
quantitative ways of making

723
00:31:06,720 --> 00:31:10,040
these trade-offs, hopefully.

724
00:31:10,040 --> 00:31:12,400
I'm going to start off with
some simple hyperparameter

725
00:31:12,400 --> 00:31:15,680
questions, things like how
do I choose my architecture

726
00:31:15,680 --> 00:31:18,000
or optimizer, things
that I talked about

727
00:31:18,000 --> 00:31:20,120
in the second lecture.

728
00:31:20,120 --> 00:31:22,640
And there I gave you
the perspective of maybe

729
00:31:22,640 --> 00:31:24,783
you can study what
other people do.

730
00:31:24,783 --> 00:31:27,200
Now I'm going to try to give
you a first-principal answers

731
00:31:27,200 --> 00:31:29,640
to those questions with a
little bit of a different set

732
00:31:29,640 --> 00:31:30,360
of trade-offs.

733
00:31:30,360 --> 00:31:32,820
So you have to believe in
scaling laws in this lecture,

734
00:31:32,820 --> 00:31:36,160
but as long as you do, you
get to try things on your own

735
00:31:36,160 --> 00:31:37,440
and find out.

736
00:31:37,440 --> 00:31:40,840
OK, the very first question
we might start with is to say,

737
00:31:40,840 --> 00:31:44,320
Are transformers really
better than LSTMs?

738
00:31:44,320 --> 00:31:45,320
LSTMs.

739
00:31:45,320 --> 00:31:46,640
They can work OK, too.

740
00:31:46,640 --> 00:31:48,500
They will fit distributions.

741
00:31:48,500 --> 00:31:50,660
With the advent of
things like Mamba,

742
00:31:50,660 --> 00:31:53,000
we know that SSMS can work.

743
00:31:53,000 --> 00:31:54,768
So are they better?

744
00:31:54,768 --> 00:31:56,560
The brute force way to
answer this question

745
00:31:56,560 --> 00:32:02,400
is to train a big LSTM GPT-3
or even bigger class of models.

746
00:32:02,400 --> 00:32:06,610
The scaling law way would be to
train a bunch of smaller models

747
00:32:06,610 --> 00:32:08,360
and say, I'm going to
train my transformer

748
00:32:08,360 --> 00:32:11,008
across a different
variety of compute ranges,

749
00:32:11,008 --> 00:32:13,550
and I'm going to train different
LSTMs with different numbers

750
00:32:13,550 --> 00:32:15,630
of layers on, once
again, a variety

751
00:32:15,630 --> 00:32:17,750
of different compute ranges.

752
00:32:17,750 --> 00:32:19,130
Now, what does this show?

753
00:32:19,130 --> 00:32:23,990
This shows LSTMs have definitely
different intercept, maybe even

754
00:32:23,990 --> 00:32:25,890
different slope than
the transformer.

755
00:32:25,890 --> 00:32:30,390
And because of this, probably
you don't want to pick out LSTM.

756
00:32:30,390 --> 00:32:33,510
This plot would justify scaling
up the transformer instead

757
00:32:33,510 --> 00:32:34,625
of the LSTM.

758
00:32:34,625 --> 00:32:36,750
And if you look at a lot
of the architecture papers

759
00:32:36,750 --> 00:32:38,250
today, like you go
look at the Mamba

760
00:32:38,250 --> 00:32:40,575
paper or the gated
delta net papers,

761
00:32:40,575 --> 00:32:41,950
they will always
have a plot that

762
00:32:41,950 --> 00:32:45,510
looks like this, where you have
vanilla transformer, our really

763
00:32:45,510 --> 00:32:46,250
cool model.

764
00:32:46,250 --> 00:32:48,843
And our really cool models,
usually either on top or below,

765
00:32:48,843 --> 00:32:51,510
because that's what would prove
that your model is doing better.

766
00:32:51,510 --> 00:32:54,310
And you certainly don't want
something where your slopes are

767
00:32:54,310 --> 00:32:56,970
worse because that means as
you scale up larger and larger,

768
00:32:56,970 --> 00:32:58,950
your models will
eventually do worse.

769
00:32:58,950 --> 00:33:01,310
So this is one way
of really reasoning

770
00:33:01,310 --> 00:33:08,020
about architectures without
training gigantic models.

771
00:33:08,020 --> 00:33:10,220
There haven't been,
I think, quite

772
00:33:10,220 --> 00:33:15,820
as many recent and good
architecture scaling law papers.

773
00:33:15,820 --> 00:33:18,980
I think the one that
is relatively recent

774
00:33:18,980 --> 00:33:22,858
and that I like quite a lot
is by Ek and others at Google,

775
00:33:22,858 --> 00:33:24,900
back when they were
publishing this kind of work,

776
00:33:24,900 --> 00:33:28,660
where they did a whole bunch
of scaling studies on T5-style

777
00:33:28,660 --> 00:33:32,660
models, where they trained
larger and larger variants of T5

778
00:33:32,660 --> 00:33:35,692
models on many
different architectures.

779
00:33:35,692 --> 00:33:37,400
And I think one thing
that's really cool,

780
00:33:37,400 --> 00:33:39,820
and one of the reasons why
I still bring this paper up,

781
00:33:39,820 --> 00:33:43,340
is they capture exactly
the architecture

782
00:33:43,340 --> 00:33:46,920
changes that we implement
today in our frontier models.

783
00:33:46,920 --> 00:33:51,620
So things like performer, which
is an efficient attention, that

784
00:33:51,620 --> 00:33:52,680
doesn't scale very well.

785
00:33:52,680 --> 00:33:55,100
We do not implement those.

786
00:33:55,100 --> 00:33:57,880
The things that we implement
are the gated linear unit.

787
00:33:57,880 --> 00:34:01,028
Red line better than green line
throughout the scaling trends.

788
00:34:01,028 --> 00:34:01,820
Switch transformer.

789
00:34:01,820 --> 00:34:03,695
I don't what happened
with their biggest run,

790
00:34:03,695 --> 00:34:07,130
but generally speaking, it's
got good scaling trends.

791
00:34:07,130 --> 00:34:09,971
And I forgot, oh,
mixture of softmaxes.

792
00:34:09,971 --> 00:34:11,429
Actually, we don't
do this anymore,

793
00:34:11,429 --> 00:34:13,389
but this is probably an
effective intervention

794
00:34:13,389 --> 00:34:15,330
given the scaling trends.

795
00:34:15,330 --> 00:34:18,370
So you see that through
this scaling trends,

796
00:34:18,370 --> 00:34:20,889
even though a lot
of these papers

797
00:34:20,889 --> 00:34:23,090
were operating on much,
much smaller compute scales

798
00:34:23,090 --> 00:34:25,250
than what we operate
today, they're

799
00:34:25,250 --> 00:34:27,530
able to see the kinds
of architecture trends

800
00:34:27,530 --> 00:34:31,429
that are driving frontier
model development back then.

801
00:34:31,429 --> 00:34:35,050
And I think this is why a lot
of people use scaling laws

802
00:34:35,050 --> 00:34:38,270
as a really, almost
paradigmatic way, saying, "Oh,

803
00:34:38,270 --> 00:34:40,300
if it doesn't show up
in the scaling law,

804
00:34:40,300 --> 00:34:41,550
it's not a good intervention."

805
00:34:41,550 --> 00:34:43,969
Because in some ways,
a lot of the past work

806
00:34:43,969 --> 00:34:47,370
has captured what we see today.

807
00:34:47,370 --> 00:34:48,690
Good.

808
00:34:48,690 --> 00:34:51,610
OK, going beyond
architectures, we

809
00:34:51,610 --> 00:34:54,250
can extend this kind of analysis
to all sorts of other things

810
00:34:54,250 --> 00:34:57,390
that we might want
to understand.

811
00:34:57,390 --> 00:35:00,613
We might ask, OK, is SGD
better or worse than ADAM?

812
00:35:00,613 --> 00:35:02,530
If you do the scaling
law analysis, this one's

813
00:35:02,530 --> 00:35:05,372
by Hestness, you'll
see very clear trends.

814
00:35:05,372 --> 00:35:07,330
This is once again, the
really mysterious thing

815
00:35:07,330 --> 00:35:11,450
of the intercepts are different,
but the slopes are very similar.

816
00:35:11,450 --> 00:35:13,490
If you fix the data
and you fix the models,

817
00:35:13,490 --> 00:35:15,190
slopes are often
very, very similar.

818
00:35:15,190 --> 00:35:17,910
I am very surprised by
this every time I see it.

819
00:35:17,910 --> 00:35:19,130
And yet, it's very true.

820
00:35:19,130 --> 00:35:21,590
It's rare to get
different slopes,

821
00:35:21,590 --> 00:35:24,010
even with an intervention
as big as SGD to ADAM.

822
00:35:24,010 --> 00:35:27,150
If you were the one
training with SGD or ADAM,

823
00:35:27,150 --> 00:35:29,130
you would think
that's a huge change.

824
00:35:29,130 --> 00:35:31,290
Scaling trends remain
roughly the same.

825
00:35:31,290 --> 00:35:33,730
In this case, this is
a language modeling

826
00:35:33,730 --> 00:35:35,190
with a recurrent highway net.

827
00:35:35,190 --> 00:35:38,170

828
00:35:38,170 --> 00:35:42,470
Similarly, in lecture 2, I
told you about aspect ratios.

829
00:35:42,470 --> 00:35:44,770
How do you pick your
depth-to-width trade-off?

830
00:35:44,770 --> 00:35:46,890
And I told you,
well, you can just

831
00:35:46,890 --> 00:35:49,990
pick some random number that's
somewhat roughly reasonable.

832
00:35:49,990 --> 00:35:52,410
It's like four times the
reasonable multiplier.

833
00:35:52,410 --> 00:35:55,310
But if you're doing this
kind of scaling analysis,

834
00:35:55,310 --> 00:35:58,030
you could much more
precisely what is happening.

835
00:35:58,030 --> 00:36:00,410
So the first thing
you could do is

836
00:36:00,410 --> 00:36:02,830
you could look at scaling
trends as a function of layers,

837
00:36:02,830 --> 00:36:05,880
and you would immediately see
that having extremely few layers

838
00:36:05,880 --> 00:36:07,460
is very, very bad
for your model.

839
00:36:07,460 --> 00:36:09,860
If you have one layer,
you're not going anywhere.

840
00:36:09,860 --> 00:36:11,920
That's a terrible,
terrible scaling trend.

841
00:36:11,920 --> 00:36:13,480
Greater than 1
layer, actually, it's

842
00:36:13,480 --> 00:36:15,600
surprisingly more
competitive, although you

843
00:36:15,600 --> 00:36:18,840
do see that at every compute
level, more layers, at least

844
00:36:18,840 --> 00:36:21,468
in this case is better.

845
00:36:21,468 --> 00:36:23,760
And if you can do a much more
fine-grained study, which

846
00:36:23,760 --> 00:36:28,440
they do in Kaplan, where
you try to identify what

847
00:36:28,440 --> 00:36:30,420
you might call
scale-invariant quantities,

848
00:36:30,420 --> 00:36:32,000
like the aspect ratio.

849
00:36:32,000 --> 00:36:34,620
The number of layers is not
a scale-invariant quantity.

850
00:36:34,620 --> 00:36:38,120
As you make your model bigger,
you do want more layers.

851
00:36:38,120 --> 00:36:40,020
But as you make
your model bigger,

852
00:36:40,020 --> 00:36:42,485
maybe the aspect
ratio, the optimal one

853
00:36:42,485 --> 00:36:43,360
should stay the same.

854
00:36:43,360 --> 00:36:45,040
This one is scale invariant.

855
00:36:45,040 --> 00:36:47,020
And so if we look
at the aspect ratio,

856
00:36:47,020 --> 00:36:49,960
we see that at
different model sizes,

857
00:36:49,960 --> 00:36:53,880
we get basically the same minima
around 100d model for every

858
00:36:53,880 --> 00:36:56,440
layer, or maybe a
little bit less.

859
00:36:56,440 --> 00:36:58,180
And it does shift
a little bit right.

860
00:36:58,180 --> 00:37:01,950
There is a shift
towards aspect ratio

861
00:37:01,950 --> 00:37:04,810
that is smaller for deeper
models, but for the most part,

862
00:37:04,810 --> 00:37:07,030
the minima stays
roughly similar.

863
00:37:07,030 --> 00:37:09,003
You can do similar
kinds of analysis

864
00:37:09,003 --> 00:37:11,170
for things like attention
head dimension and others.

865
00:37:11,170 --> 00:37:12,910
And if you look at
the Kaplan paper,

866
00:37:12,910 --> 00:37:15,330
they do quite a good job
at doing all of this.

867
00:37:15,330 --> 00:37:18,270
And this is one way that you can
be confident about your scaling

868
00:37:18,270 --> 00:37:19,030
strategy.

869
00:37:19,030 --> 00:37:21,870
So if your scaling strategy
is "I'm going to fix my aspect

870
00:37:21,870 --> 00:37:23,410
ratio, and I'm
going to scale up,"

871
00:37:23,410 --> 00:37:26,670
you can make plots like this and
convince yourself we're probably

872
00:37:26,670 --> 00:37:29,790
good because our optimization
shifting too much as I go

873
00:37:29,790 --> 00:37:31,690
to larger and larger
and larger models.

874
00:37:31,690 --> 00:37:34,910

875
00:37:34,910 --> 00:37:39,110
One thing that's important, and
this is a huge can of worms,

876
00:37:39,110 --> 00:37:40,750
and a sufficiently
big can of worms

877
00:37:40,750 --> 00:37:43,210
that I will talk about it
on more than one slide,

878
00:37:43,210 --> 00:37:47,410
I will come back, not all
parameters are created equal.

879
00:37:47,410 --> 00:37:49,690
And because not all
parameters are created equal,

880
00:37:49,690 --> 00:37:51,910
your scaling laws
may look good or bad

881
00:37:51,910 --> 00:37:55,870
depending on how you
define what a parameter is.

882
00:37:55,870 --> 00:37:58,790
So in the Kaplan paper, they
made this observation that

883
00:37:58,790 --> 00:38:02,900
if they drew their scaling laws
for depth with the embedding

884
00:38:02,900 --> 00:38:05,540
parameters, they got these very
funky-looking scaling laws,

885
00:38:05,540 --> 00:38:08,245
and they said, "Oh, this is no
good at all, no good at all."

886
00:38:08,245 --> 00:38:09,620
Because it's no
good at all, they

887
00:38:09,620 --> 00:38:11,780
decided that they
were going to exclude

888
00:38:11,780 --> 00:38:14,300
all the embedding parameters,
and they were only

889
00:38:14,300 --> 00:38:17,520
going to count the non-embedding
parameters because you could

890
00:38:17,520 --> 00:38:19,020
justify it to
yourself, saying these

891
00:38:19,020 --> 00:38:23,363
are the parameters that are
doing computation or whatever.

892
00:38:23,363 --> 00:38:24,780
We're going to see
later that this

893
00:38:24,780 --> 00:38:27,620
is going to have non-trivial
implications on the validity

894
00:38:27,620 --> 00:38:28,960
of some of the results.

895
00:38:28,960 --> 00:38:31,720
But the point here is
scaling laws aren't magic.

896
00:38:31,720 --> 00:38:33,820
I think Percy likes
to make this point,

897
00:38:33,820 --> 00:38:36,940
that scaling laws and all
these kinds of predictability

898
00:38:36,940 --> 00:38:38,700
across scales is engineered.

899
00:38:38,700 --> 00:38:40,480
They don't happen automatically.

900
00:38:40,480 --> 00:38:43,677
We need to pick the right
kinds of x-axes to look at.

901
00:38:43,677 --> 00:38:46,260
We need to make sure that the
hyperparameters for these things

902
00:38:46,260 --> 00:38:48,620
are set right, and only
under those conditions

903
00:38:48,620 --> 00:38:51,300
does it become possible
to get predictable scaling

904
00:38:51,300 --> 00:38:53,480
across many orders
of magnitude compute.

905
00:38:53,480 --> 00:38:57,520
So this is what a lot of
scaling law researchers do.

906
00:38:57,520 --> 00:38:59,140
They think about
what the right way

907
00:38:59,140 --> 00:39:01,980
is to get very
predictable scaling.

908
00:39:01,980 --> 00:39:04,380
Now, related to
this, I think are

909
00:39:04,380 --> 00:39:07,780
very interesting recent
papers on things like scaling

910
00:39:07,780 --> 00:39:09,460
for mixture of experts.

911
00:39:09,460 --> 00:39:11,960
Now, that mixture of
experts is, you would say,

912
00:39:11,960 --> 00:39:15,380
the dominant way of
training large models,

913
00:39:15,380 --> 00:39:18,020
it is quite important to
think about what is the value

914
00:39:18,020 --> 00:39:20,180
or what is a parameter
in this new world

915
00:39:20,180 --> 00:39:22,860
where the number of parameters
and the number of parameters

916
00:39:22,860 --> 00:39:24,500
are kind of decoupled.

917
00:39:24,500 --> 00:39:26,780
And there's a nice,
clean analysis

918
00:39:26,780 --> 00:39:30,460
by some folks at
Apple and MIT that

919
00:39:30,460 --> 00:39:36,220
show the scaling trends as a
function of both parameters

920
00:39:36,220 --> 00:39:37,640
and active parameters.

921
00:39:37,640 --> 00:39:40,140
And the cool thing
that you see is,

922
00:39:40,140 --> 00:39:43,040
as you start to train
bigger and bigger models,

923
00:39:43,040 --> 00:39:45,460
if you increase the
amount of total parameters

924
00:39:45,460 --> 00:39:49,220
on the x-axis over here, and
you want to minimize your loss,

925
00:39:49,220 --> 00:39:52,240
you're going to end up with
more sparse and sparse models.

926
00:39:52,240 --> 00:39:55,340
See how the color becomes
darker and darker and darker.

927
00:39:55,340 --> 00:39:59,530
Similarly, here, if you like
the number of active parameters

928
00:39:59,530 --> 00:40:03,370
decreases as a function
of MoE sparsity.

929
00:40:03,370 --> 00:40:05,730
And you get the
result that I think

930
00:40:05,730 --> 00:40:07,450
is somewhat
intuitive here, which

931
00:40:07,450 --> 00:40:09,450
is you can draw these surfaces.

932
00:40:09,450 --> 00:40:12,890
For every compute
amount, you can ask, OK,

933
00:40:12,890 --> 00:40:17,850
would I rather spend that
compute on active parameters

934
00:40:17,850 --> 00:40:19,770
and how much sparsity do I want?

935
00:40:19,770 --> 00:40:23,210
I can vary my sparsity without
really changing the amount

936
00:40:23,210 --> 00:40:25,290
of compute that I'm spending.

937
00:40:25,290 --> 00:40:27,130
Now, given that you
can ask, OK, what

938
00:40:27,130 --> 00:40:29,590
happens if I add more
of empty parameters,

939
00:40:29,590 --> 00:40:32,250
same active parameters,
but more total parameters?

940
00:40:32,250 --> 00:40:34,570
As you move from
back on the plot

941
00:40:34,570 --> 00:40:39,090
to in the front of the plot, you
see the losses are improving.

942
00:40:39,090 --> 00:40:41,330
So in other words,
this is saying

943
00:40:41,330 --> 00:40:43,810
that the parameters in an
MoE that are not active

944
00:40:43,810 --> 00:40:45,843
are still helping you
reduce your loss, which

945
00:40:45,843 --> 00:40:46,510
is kind of cool.

946
00:40:46,510 --> 00:40:49,110
And you can write down what the
functional forms of this are.

947
00:40:49,110 --> 00:40:51,570
I won't go into that
part in more detail,

948
00:40:51,570 --> 00:40:54,890
but the point here is to say
all of these kinds of quantities

949
00:40:54,890 --> 00:40:57,440
that we care about
in optimizing MoE

950
00:40:57,440 --> 00:41:00,620
are things that have predictable
scaling and regularities.

951
00:41:00,620 --> 00:41:03,700

952
00:41:03,700 --> 00:41:07,960
And the two things I want to now
talk about are actually really--

953
00:41:07,960 --> 00:41:10,400
yeah, two things that I
now want to talk about.

954
00:41:10,400 --> 00:41:12,760
When you're going to
train a new big model,

955
00:41:12,760 --> 00:41:15,200
actually, most of the things
that I talk about are fixed.

956
00:41:15,200 --> 00:41:16,920
You're probably all
not radical enough

957
00:41:16,920 --> 00:41:18,340
that you're going
to switch to a LSTM.

958
00:41:18,340 --> 00:41:20,382
You're probably going to
pick like, I don't know,

959
00:41:20,382 --> 00:41:23,960
a DeepSeek-V4-inspired
MoE or something.

960
00:41:23,960 --> 00:41:26,880
And you're probably going to
pick architecture choices that

961
00:41:26,880 --> 00:41:27,980
are tried and tested.

962
00:41:27,980 --> 00:41:30,000
You're not going to really
throw everything out.

963
00:41:30,000 --> 00:41:31,840
But two things
that you really do

964
00:41:31,840 --> 00:41:34,440
have to maybe
reinvent in some sense

965
00:41:34,440 --> 00:41:36,600
is the batch size and
the learning rate.

966
00:41:36,600 --> 00:41:38,400
If you train your
deep learning models,

967
00:41:38,400 --> 00:41:41,160
you know that you have
to be very careful

968
00:41:41,160 --> 00:41:43,920
about the combination of your
batch size and learning rate.

969
00:41:43,920 --> 00:41:45,880
You change one, you have
to change the other.

970
00:41:45,880 --> 00:41:48,880
Now, batch size, from
the previous lecture,

971
00:41:48,880 --> 00:41:51,020
is very tricky for
another reason.

972
00:41:51,020 --> 00:41:53,180
We want the batch size
to be as big as possible

973
00:41:53,180 --> 00:41:55,350
because a big batch size
gives us opportunity

974
00:41:55,350 --> 00:41:56,430
for parallelization.

975
00:41:56,430 --> 00:41:59,910
Remember, data parallel
requires large batch size.

976
00:41:59,910 --> 00:42:02,550
So now, having said all
that, what we really want to

977
00:42:02,550 --> 00:42:05,790
is how large can we
make our batch size

978
00:42:05,790 --> 00:42:07,130
before we start to suffer?

979
00:42:07,130 --> 00:42:10,150
That's the relevant
systems question.

980
00:42:10,150 --> 00:42:12,350
And you also want to
how does that change

981
00:42:12,350 --> 00:42:14,030
as a function of model size.

982
00:42:14,030 --> 00:42:19,750
So given that preface, there
is this very important idea

983
00:42:19,750 --> 00:42:21,615
that is used in a
variety of papers

984
00:42:21,615 --> 00:42:22,990
and talked about
very frequently,

985
00:42:22,990 --> 00:42:24,990
called the critical batch size.

986
00:42:24,990 --> 00:42:27,510
The critical batch size
is roughly this idea that

987
00:42:27,510 --> 00:42:29,230
up until a certain
point, when you

988
00:42:29,230 --> 00:42:31,010
start to get
diminishing returns,

989
00:42:31,010 --> 00:42:33,270
there is returns,
perfect returns,

990
00:42:33,270 --> 00:42:34,610
to increased batch size.

991
00:42:34,610 --> 00:42:38,170
So I might call this the
noise-limited regime.

992
00:42:38,170 --> 00:42:40,870
So in the noise-limited regime,
every additional element

993
00:42:40,870 --> 00:42:44,670
that you throw in your batch
reduces the gradient noise

994
00:42:44,670 --> 00:42:45,770
in your SGD step.

995
00:42:45,770 --> 00:42:48,230
And since you're
variance-limited,

996
00:42:48,230 --> 00:42:50,770
that reduction in variance
is very, very helpful.

997
00:42:50,770 --> 00:42:52,600
That gives you big returns.

998
00:42:52,600 --> 00:42:55,000
So this is the regime in which
you have perfect scaling.

999
00:42:55,000 --> 00:42:57,100
You're doing about
as well as you can,

1000
00:42:57,100 --> 00:43:00,940
given the amount of extra
examples that you're processing.

1001
00:43:00,940 --> 00:43:02,660
As you get to a
certain point where

1002
00:43:02,660 --> 00:43:05,660
you've reached the noise
scale of your gradients,

1003
00:43:05,660 --> 00:43:08,880
you get to a point where you
are no longer variance-limited.

1004
00:43:08,880 --> 00:43:10,320
You're now bias-limited.

1005
00:43:10,320 --> 00:43:11,360
What does that mean?

1006
00:43:11,360 --> 00:43:13,000
Well, remember, in
gradient descent,

1007
00:43:13,000 --> 00:43:15,480
you're looking at the local
structure of your objective.

1008
00:43:15,480 --> 00:43:19,520
You don't have a global view
of where the minimum is.

1009
00:43:19,520 --> 00:43:22,580
So no matter how
low noise I am, I'm

1010
00:43:22,580 --> 00:43:25,040
only going to point in this
light blue direction over here.

1011
00:43:25,040 --> 00:43:26,540
And there's always
a disagreement

1012
00:43:26,540 --> 00:43:28,380
between my local
descent direction

1013
00:43:28,380 --> 00:43:30,900
and where the global optimum is.

1014
00:43:30,900 --> 00:43:32,780
Because of that,
at a certain point,

1015
00:43:32,780 --> 00:43:34,300
I'm going to get
diminishing returns

1016
00:43:34,300 --> 00:43:37,800
because now my limiting
factor is no longer variance.

1017
00:43:37,800 --> 00:43:39,400
It is this bias term.

1018
00:43:39,400 --> 00:43:44,100
So now I'm bias-limited
past this point over here.

1019
00:43:44,100 --> 00:43:46,940
And so critical batch is
kind of a rule of thumb,

1020
00:43:46,940 --> 00:43:51,340
you might call it, or a
convenient trade-off point where

1021
00:43:51,340 --> 00:43:53,540
you say this is the point
at which we're starting

1022
00:43:53,540 --> 00:43:55,780
to cross over from
our perfect scaling

1023
00:43:55,780 --> 00:43:59,140
regime to our ineffective
scaling regime.

1024
00:43:59,140 --> 00:44:01,097
And so it gives you
a good rule of thumb

1025
00:44:01,097 --> 00:44:03,180
of where to be setting
your batch size if you want

1026
00:44:03,180 --> 00:44:05,860
your batch size to be as big
as possible without suffering

1027
00:44:05,860 --> 00:44:08,480
these huge efficiency losses.

1028
00:44:08,480 --> 00:44:11,340
Hopefully, that
idea is very clear.

1029
00:44:11,340 --> 00:44:16,060
Now, critical batch size is
kind of a strange object.

1030
00:44:16,060 --> 00:44:20,860
It is derived from a variety
of complicated calculations

1031
00:44:20,860 --> 00:44:24,940
about the local quadratic
approximations to the objective.

1032
00:44:24,940 --> 00:44:28,020
But I will maybe give
you the mechanical steps

1033
00:44:28,020 --> 00:44:32,420
of what is actually used
to estimate this quantity.

1034
00:44:32,420 --> 00:44:36,180
So what you do is first,
you pick a target loss.

1035
00:44:36,180 --> 00:44:38,480
Target loss might be some
numbers that you want to hit.

1036
00:44:38,480 --> 00:44:40,580
So your goal is to
hit this target loss

1037
00:44:40,580 --> 00:44:41,860
as quickly as possible.

1038
00:44:41,860 --> 00:44:44,020
That's my goal right now.

1039
00:44:44,020 --> 00:44:45,820
What I'm going to do
is I'm going to sweep

1040
00:44:45,820 --> 00:44:47,980
over all the different
batch sizes or number

1041
00:44:47,980 --> 00:44:49,690
of different batch
sizes, and I'm

1042
00:44:49,690 --> 00:44:52,130
going to write down the
number of steps that I needed

1043
00:44:52,130 --> 00:44:54,730
to reach my target loss,
and the number of examples

1044
00:44:54,730 --> 00:44:57,310
that were needed to get
to that target loss.

1045
00:44:57,310 --> 00:44:59,330
Of course, number
one and two are

1046
00:44:59,330 --> 00:45:01,250
related by a batch size factor.

1047
00:45:01,250 --> 00:45:03,290
The number of steps
times my batch size

1048
00:45:03,290 --> 00:45:05,810
is equal to the
number of examples.

1049
00:45:05,810 --> 00:45:11,250
Now, through some calculations,
OpenAI folks, McCandless

1050
00:45:11,250 --> 00:45:15,810
and others, argue that this
is the rough functional form

1051
00:45:15,810 --> 00:45:19,010
that we should follow, that the
number of steps that you need

1052
00:45:19,010 --> 00:45:20,730
and the number of
examples that you need

1053
00:45:20,730 --> 00:45:24,090
are inversely related,
with this estimate, which

1054
00:45:24,090 --> 00:45:26,170
is the minimum number
of possible steps

1055
00:45:26,170 --> 00:45:28,890
and the minimum
number of examples,

1056
00:45:28,890 --> 00:45:31,990
normalizing these
two quantities.

1057
00:45:31,990 --> 00:45:34,052
And so there's a trade-off
between these two.

1058
00:45:34,052 --> 00:45:36,010
If you want to minimize
the number of examples,

1059
00:45:36,010 --> 00:45:37,550
you're going to
have a lot of steps.

1060
00:45:37,550 --> 00:45:39,170
And if you want to minimize
the number of steps,

1061
00:45:39,170 --> 00:45:40,795
you're going to need
a lot of examples.

1062
00:45:40,795 --> 00:45:42,190
That's a very natural trade-off.

1063
00:45:42,190 --> 00:45:44,690
And if you increase or
decrease your batch size,

1064
00:45:44,690 --> 00:45:48,000
you're going to have trade-offs
that go one side or the other.

1065
00:45:48,000 --> 00:45:49,960
Now, they argue
the goal now should

1066
00:45:49,960 --> 00:45:51,580
be to balance these two sides.

1067
00:45:51,580 --> 00:45:54,080
I want to make sure that
my number of steps term

1068
00:45:54,080 --> 00:45:56,560
and the number of
examples terms are roughly

1069
00:45:56,560 --> 00:45:58,020
balanced with each other.

1070
00:45:58,020 --> 00:45:59,480
And if you solve
for that, you're

1071
00:45:59,480 --> 00:46:01,640
going to get this
particular solution, which

1072
00:46:01,640 --> 00:46:04,480
is that the critical
batch size is

1073
00:46:04,480 --> 00:46:06,640
the minimum number
of examples that I

1074
00:46:06,640 --> 00:46:09,940
need divided by the minimum
number of steps that I need.

1075
00:46:09,940 --> 00:46:14,120
And these two are estimated by
fitting this to the observed

1076
00:46:14,120 --> 00:46:15,653
data that I have.

1077
00:46:15,653 --> 00:46:17,820
This is going to balance
both sides of the equation.

1078
00:46:17,820 --> 00:46:20,920
It's going to give me a little
bit more number of steps

1079
00:46:20,920 --> 00:46:21,620
than optimal.

1080
00:46:21,620 --> 00:46:24,700
It's going to require a little
bit more examples than optimal,

1081
00:46:24,700 --> 00:46:26,100
but it balances the two sides.

1082
00:46:26,100 --> 00:46:32,640
And you can think of this as
optimizing the convergence rate

1083
00:46:32,640 --> 00:46:37,000
without blowing up the number
of steps that you need.

1084
00:46:37,000 --> 00:46:39,375
There's another added
point that I won't really

1085
00:46:39,375 --> 00:46:41,500
belabor because this gets
even more into the weeds.

1086
00:46:41,500 --> 00:46:43,680
You should really read the
critical batch size paper

1087
00:46:43,680 --> 00:46:46,390
if you're interested into this,
where one of their arguments

1088
00:46:46,390 --> 00:46:48,690
is estimating this
is quite complicated.

1089
00:46:48,690 --> 00:46:51,430
You can do this by just
looking at the ratio

1090
00:46:51,430 --> 00:46:53,470
of the gradient covariance
in the squared norm

1091
00:46:53,470 --> 00:46:56,390
of your gradient.

1092
00:46:56,390 --> 00:46:59,370
So why is that even in
the scaling lecture?

1093
00:46:59,370 --> 00:47:02,710
This is a thing that
is not really related

1094
00:47:02,710 --> 00:47:04,550
to scaling so far.

1095
00:47:04,550 --> 00:47:07,270
The reason why this is
in the scaling lecture

1096
00:47:07,270 --> 00:47:09,590
is because, first of all,
the critical batch size

1097
00:47:09,590 --> 00:47:11,370
is a reasonable
batch size to pick.

1098
00:47:11,370 --> 00:47:13,550
That's a batch size that
balances convergence rate

1099
00:47:13,550 --> 00:47:15,110
and the size of the batch.

1100
00:47:15,110 --> 00:47:19,070
So if I want to pick my batch
size equal to my critical batch

1101
00:47:19,070 --> 00:47:21,310
size, you might ask,
how does this thing

1102
00:47:21,310 --> 00:47:24,370
scale as a function
of my target loss?

1103
00:47:24,370 --> 00:47:27,350
Target loss can be
thought of as compute.

1104
00:47:27,350 --> 00:47:30,750
If I want to have a model that
is better and better and better,

1105
00:47:30,750 --> 00:47:33,690
what is the batch size
that I should use?

1106
00:47:33,690 --> 00:47:36,990
And the reason why this
isn't the scaling law lecture

1107
00:47:36,990 --> 00:47:40,910
is as you go down in scale
in the loss, so your loss is

1108
00:47:40,910 --> 00:47:44,430
improving, the amount of
critical batch size increases.

1109
00:47:44,430 --> 00:47:46,750
And it increases in this
very predictable way, which

1110
00:47:46,750 --> 00:47:48,590
is once again, a power law.

1111
00:47:48,590 --> 00:47:51,230
So this is the relationship
that they argue,

1112
00:47:51,230 --> 00:47:56,430
which is roughly inverse
polynomial in batch size.

1113
00:47:56,430 --> 00:47:59,080
So given this, this
is actually quite nice

1114
00:47:59,080 --> 00:48:00,830
because what this is
saying is if you have

1115
00:48:00,830 --> 00:48:02,910
a really large-scale
training run where you go all

1116
00:48:02,910 --> 00:48:04,327
the way to the
right of this plot,

1117
00:48:04,327 --> 00:48:06,590
your batch sizes
can be quite large.

1118
00:48:06,590 --> 00:48:07,670
And that makes sense.

1119
00:48:07,670 --> 00:48:10,810
As you get closer and closer
and closer to the minimum,

1120
00:48:10,810 --> 00:48:13,028
the value of variance
reduction increases.

1121
00:48:13,028 --> 00:48:15,070
Because you're trying to
optimize finer and finer

1122
00:48:15,070 --> 00:48:17,030
and finer grained
objects, the noise

1123
00:48:17,030 --> 00:48:19,870
matters a lot more once you're
minimizing really, really

1124
00:48:19,870 --> 00:48:22,590
tiny differences.

1125
00:48:22,590 --> 00:48:25,990
So that's the batch size story.

1126
00:48:25,990 --> 00:48:27,950
The other side-- so
the two quantities,

1127
00:48:27,950 --> 00:48:30,150
remember, that you're going
to always have to tune

1128
00:48:30,150 --> 00:48:32,830
is batch size and learning rate.

1129
00:48:32,830 --> 00:48:34,870
The learning rate
side of the story

1130
00:48:34,870 --> 00:48:36,530
is also a little
bit complicated.

1131
00:48:36,530 --> 00:48:40,310
I'll go into this much more
detail in the advanced scaling

1132
00:48:40,310 --> 00:48:42,390
lecture.

1133
00:48:42,390 --> 00:48:44,820
But learning rates
generally shift.

1134
00:48:44,820 --> 00:48:46,940
I think this is a good
mental picture for how

1135
00:48:46,940 --> 00:48:49,460
to think about learning
rates in the standard world

1136
00:48:49,460 --> 00:48:51,540
that you start with,
which is, let's say,

1137
00:48:51,540 --> 00:48:53,300
that you have just like a MLP.

1138
00:48:53,300 --> 00:48:55,860
Just a standard neural
network of some kind.

1139
00:48:55,860 --> 00:48:57,360
I'm not going to
do depth scaling.

1140
00:48:57,360 --> 00:48:59,020
I'm only going to
do width scaling.

1141
00:48:59,020 --> 00:49:01,140
Now, if I'm doing width
scaling, the bigger

1142
00:49:01,140 --> 00:49:03,820
my model, the bigger
my model, the smaller

1143
00:49:03,820 --> 00:49:05,240
my learning rate should be.

1144
00:49:05,240 --> 00:49:07,580
And the reason why my learning
rate should be smaller

1145
00:49:07,580 --> 00:49:10,000
is because I have
bigger, more parameters,

1146
00:49:10,000 --> 00:49:11,480
I'm changing more
things at once.

1147
00:49:11,480 --> 00:49:13,460
Maybe I should move less.

1148
00:49:13,460 --> 00:49:15,580
It kind of makes sense.

1149
00:49:15,580 --> 00:49:18,660
And in fact, there's
a rule of thumb

1150
00:49:18,660 --> 00:49:21,160
that maybe you want to
scale by 1 over the width.

1151
00:49:21,160 --> 00:49:23,460
That's a very commonly
known rule of thumb

1152
00:49:23,460 --> 00:49:26,900
that you want to just decrease
the learning rate regularly

1153
00:49:26,900 --> 00:49:28,540
as a function of
your width, and that

1154
00:49:28,540 --> 00:49:32,340
will give you a rule of thumb
to scale your learning rate.

1155
00:49:32,340 --> 00:49:35,620
There is another
school of thought,

1156
00:49:35,620 --> 00:49:38,100
or another set of
tricks that people do,

1157
00:49:38,100 --> 00:49:40,260
which is that
people also rescale

1158
00:49:40,260 --> 00:49:41,710
the network in various ways.

1159
00:49:41,710 --> 00:49:43,810
They will change the
initialization sizes,

1160
00:49:43,810 --> 00:49:46,970
and they will change
the step sizes

1161
00:49:46,970 --> 00:49:50,650
of the optimizer for
various parts of the network

1162
00:49:50,650 --> 00:49:55,250
in order to force the model
to have the same learning rate

1163
00:49:55,250 --> 00:49:57,410
minimum across scales.

1164
00:49:57,410 --> 00:50:03,105
And this is an idea that is
called mu p and others like it.

1165
00:50:03,105 --> 00:50:04,730
Some people have
reported great success

1166
00:50:04,730 --> 00:50:06,850
with these kinds of
approaches, others

1167
00:50:06,850 --> 00:50:08,450
have reported less success.

1168
00:50:08,450 --> 00:50:11,650
The two ways, basically,
of setting a learning rate

1169
00:50:11,650 --> 00:50:14,750
for large-scale runs really
is captured in this plot.

1170
00:50:14,750 --> 00:50:16,370
In one way, you
can try to estimate

1171
00:50:16,370 --> 00:50:18,290
what these minima are
and try to predict

1172
00:50:18,290 --> 00:50:19,890
what your minimum will be.

1173
00:50:19,890 --> 00:50:22,590
And the way in which the minimum
changes is pretty predictable,

1174
00:50:22,590 --> 00:50:24,330
so this is not a crazy idea.

1175
00:50:24,330 --> 00:50:26,570
Or you can
reparameterize your model

1176
00:50:26,570 --> 00:50:28,910
to try to keep the learning
rate minimum the same,

1177
00:50:28,910 --> 00:50:31,390
and then just pick your
best learning rate and go.

1178
00:50:31,390 --> 00:50:34,670
Those are the two philosophies
to picking your learning rate.

1179
00:50:34,670 --> 00:50:37,630
Both of them have been
applied successfully at scale.

1180
00:50:37,630 --> 00:50:39,170
There are large-scale
training runs

1181
00:50:39,170 --> 00:50:41,560
that have used both approaches.

1182
00:50:41,560 --> 00:50:46,520
I think anecdotally it does seem
more people are maybe favoring

1183
00:50:46,520 --> 00:50:49,560
the scaling law approach,
but both are certainly

1184
00:50:49,560 --> 00:50:50,800
viable approaches.

1185
00:50:50,800 --> 00:50:55,600
I'll talk in detail about
both of these next lecture.

1186
00:50:55,600 --> 00:51:01,640
So before I get into the
last part of this lecture,

1187
00:51:01,640 --> 00:51:03,640
where I want to
talk about not quite

1188
00:51:03,640 --> 00:51:09,320
pitfalls but various details
of scaling law things,

1189
00:51:09,320 --> 00:51:11,160
one thing I want to
end this section with

1190
00:51:11,160 --> 00:51:14,240
is the idea that upstream
and downstream performance

1191
00:51:14,240 --> 00:51:15,960
can be quite different.

1192
00:51:15,960 --> 00:51:19,680
So if you look at something
like log likelihood,

1193
00:51:19,680 --> 00:51:23,360
this is from the Yi Tay
architectures paper,

1194
00:51:23,360 --> 00:51:28,080
you find that perplexity and
negative perplexity in this case

1195
00:51:28,080 --> 00:51:29,900
parameters are very correlated.

1196
00:51:29,900 --> 00:51:32,420
The more parameters you
have, the better your model,

1197
00:51:32,420 --> 00:51:33,260
very predictably.

1198
00:51:33,260 --> 00:51:35,640
This is a very
beautiful linear trend.

1199
00:51:35,640 --> 00:51:37,375
Now you're like,
OK, this is great.

1200
00:51:37,375 --> 00:51:39,000
Now we're going to
ship our best model.

1201
00:51:39,000 --> 00:51:40,360
NL12.

1202
00:51:40,360 --> 00:51:42,420
Turns out NL12 is
not your best model.

1203
00:51:42,420 --> 00:51:45,600
It was actually NL32XL,
which was a much worse model

1204
00:51:45,600 --> 00:51:46,640
in perplexity.

1205
00:51:46,640 --> 00:51:48,800
This is a very common
thing that does happen.

1206
00:51:48,800 --> 00:51:51,603
Upstream and downstream
are often correlated.

1207
00:51:51,603 --> 00:51:53,520
This is probably one of
the worst correlations

1208
00:51:53,520 --> 00:51:55,378
that I've seen from
upstream to downstream.

1209
00:51:55,378 --> 00:51:56,920
But I think it's
very, very important

1210
00:51:56,920 --> 00:52:00,440
to remember that scaling laws,
generally speaking, are objects

1211
00:52:00,440 --> 00:52:02,500
that you want to apply
on the perplexity side.

1212
00:52:02,500 --> 00:52:03,860
They're very clean,
they're very regular,

1213
00:52:03,860 --> 00:52:05,040
they're very predictable.

1214
00:52:05,040 --> 00:52:07,400
But transfer from
perplexity to downstream

1215
00:52:07,400 --> 00:52:09,880
is a lot less certain than
it might initially seem.

1216
00:52:09,880 --> 00:52:13,080
This is a place where you
want to be very cautious.

1217
00:52:13,080 --> 00:52:17,040
And as an anecdote, I
have former students

1218
00:52:17,040 --> 00:52:19,580
that have gone and done
post-training of various places,

1219
00:52:19,580 --> 00:52:20,380
and they always complain.

1220
00:52:20,380 --> 00:52:22,120
They're like, "Oh, those
pre-training people,

1221
00:52:22,120 --> 00:52:23,120
they hand you this model.

1222
00:52:23,120 --> 00:52:24,260
They are like, the
perplexity is good.

1223
00:52:24,260 --> 00:52:25,580
It's all your problem now."

1224
00:52:25,580 --> 00:52:27,760
But it's often the
problems have started

1225
00:52:27,760 --> 00:52:29,140
at the pre-training side.

1226
00:52:29,140 --> 00:52:31,060
So you don't want to
be one of those people.

1227
00:52:31,060 --> 00:52:33,060
You want to really think
about transfer as well,

1228
00:52:33,060 --> 00:52:36,990
not just focus on perplexities.

1229
00:52:36,990 --> 00:52:39,970
OK, so maybe this is
surprising, maybe this is not.

1230
00:52:39,970 --> 00:52:42,230
But the point that I
want to drill in here

1231
00:52:42,230 --> 00:52:45,130
is that before you do
your big training run,

1232
00:52:45,130 --> 00:52:47,510
you should have a rough idea
of what you're going to see.

1233
00:52:47,510 --> 00:52:50,350
Not just is it going
to train at all.

1234
00:52:50,350 --> 00:52:51,870
In fact, if you do
scaling laws, you

1235
00:52:51,870 --> 00:52:55,310
should be able to actually
predict fairly precisely

1236
00:52:55,310 --> 00:52:58,070
the numerical values,
almost, of how good

1237
00:52:58,070 --> 00:52:59,110
your run is going to be.

1238
00:52:59,110 --> 00:53:01,250
If I choose one optimizer
versus the other,

1239
00:53:01,250 --> 00:53:02,370
what will the gain be?

1240
00:53:02,370 --> 00:53:04,990
You should be able
to predict that.

1241
00:53:04,990 --> 00:53:06,670
So the scaling
law-based procedure

1242
00:53:06,670 --> 00:53:09,250
is to say I'm going to train
a bunch of smaller models,

1243
00:53:09,250 --> 00:53:11,670
I'm going to establish
some of a scaling law

1244
00:53:11,670 --> 00:53:14,210
by fitting these lines
onto log-log plots.

1245
00:53:14,210 --> 00:53:15,890
And then if the
fit is good enough,

1246
00:53:15,890 --> 00:53:18,015
I'm going to have some
confidence that the gap will

1247
00:53:18,015 --> 00:53:20,310
persist, and then I'm
just going to deploy that

1248
00:53:20,310 --> 00:53:21,910
onto my large-scale
training run.

1249
00:53:21,910 --> 00:53:24,970
This is the naive version
of a scaling law procedure,

1250
00:53:24,970 --> 00:53:26,790
but I think one
that is reasonably

1251
00:53:26,790 --> 00:53:29,970
accurate for how you might think
about these things in practice.

1252
00:53:29,970 --> 00:53:31,390
What else are you going to do?

1253
00:53:31,390 --> 00:53:33,610
Just deploy a run
out of nowhere?

1254
00:53:33,610 --> 00:53:35,860
Probably not.

1255
00:53:35,860 --> 00:53:37,940
OK, I can pause here
and take some questions

1256
00:53:37,940 --> 00:53:42,140
before I get into the
last part of this lecture.

1257
00:53:42,140 --> 00:53:43,380
Yes.

1258
00:53:43,380 --> 00:53:45,520
For each of the data
points on the scaling law,

1259
00:53:45,520 --> 00:53:47,320
how do you think about variance?

1260
00:53:47,320 --> 00:53:49,320
How many runs do you do?

1261
00:53:49,320 --> 00:53:51,940
And is aggregation
usually like the average,

1262
00:53:51,940 --> 00:53:54,660
or do you take min or max
depending on [INAUDIBLE]?

1263
00:53:54,660 --> 00:53:59,820
Yeah, I think in most cases, if
not all cases that I've seen,

1264
00:53:59,820 --> 00:54:01,380
those are singletons.

1265
00:54:01,380 --> 00:54:03,420
And the reason why
it's a singleton

1266
00:54:03,420 --> 00:54:06,060
is because when you're
looking at perplexities,

1267
00:54:06,060 --> 00:54:08,480
perplexities are
very, very clean.

1268
00:54:08,480 --> 00:54:12,282
These objects, if
you rerun them,

1269
00:54:12,282 --> 00:54:13,740
they're probably
like the variances

1270
00:54:13,740 --> 00:54:16,340
in the second decimal
place or something

1271
00:54:16,340 --> 00:54:18,340
because the training
data is very

1272
00:54:18,340 --> 00:54:21,740
homogeneous, you've got a lot of
it, your eval sets are very big.

1273
00:54:21,740 --> 00:54:23,740
You've really pushed the
variance very, very low

1274
00:54:23,740 --> 00:54:25,500
on these things.

1275
00:54:25,500 --> 00:54:30,020
But if you're doing something
learning rate or critical batch

1276
00:54:30,020 --> 00:54:34,010
size scaling laws, you will see
some truly horrendous stuff.

1277
00:54:34,010 --> 00:54:36,350
I do think people do
some variance reduction,

1278
00:54:36,350 --> 00:54:38,770
but it's actually not
that common as common

1279
00:54:38,770 --> 00:54:41,970
as it should be, maybe.

1280
00:54:41,970 --> 00:54:43,702
Yes.

1281
00:54:43,702 --> 00:54:47,930
Do people use just
downstream metrics directly

1282
00:54:47,930 --> 00:54:49,570
instead of using perplexity?

1283
00:54:49,570 --> 00:54:52,830
Yeah, people do scaling laws
directly on downstream metrics,

1284
00:54:52,830 --> 00:54:54,770
but that has the
same problem as this.

1285
00:54:54,770 --> 00:54:58,290
So I think the way to
think about scaling laws

1286
00:54:58,290 --> 00:55:01,420
is to really reliably
establish at least one thing.

1287
00:55:01,420 --> 00:55:03,170
So the way to think
about it is, let's say

1288
00:55:03,170 --> 00:55:06,970
I see something
like this, I have

1289
00:55:06,970 --> 00:55:08,627
a strong belief that
this transformer

1290
00:55:08,627 --> 00:55:09,710
line is going to continue.

1291
00:55:09,710 --> 00:55:11,745
This is a very regular trend.

1292
00:55:11,745 --> 00:55:13,370
And it might curve
a little bit, but we

1293
00:55:13,370 --> 00:55:15,328
do think there's probably
some regularity here.

1294
00:55:15,328 --> 00:55:18,490
But instead, imagine this
was a very noisy jaggedy line

1295
00:55:18,490 --> 00:55:19,510
of this form.

1296
00:55:19,510 --> 00:55:22,070
You would not really
know what is going on.

1297
00:55:22,070 --> 00:55:24,190
You would say, I maybe
believe that it will go on,

1298
00:55:24,190 --> 00:55:26,450
but maybe it's a line,
maybe it's a curve.

1299
00:55:26,450 --> 00:55:28,090
So I think the general--

1300
00:55:28,090 --> 00:55:30,630
if I'm trying to explain the
philosophy of this stuff,

1301
00:55:30,630 --> 00:55:33,370
is to say, OK, let's start with
the low variance measurement

1302
00:55:33,370 --> 00:55:37,010
and then establish scaling
regularity there and then rely

1303
00:55:37,010 --> 00:55:40,690
on some of belief about
transfer or establish transfer

1304
00:55:40,690 --> 00:55:43,570
or elsewhere to try to show
that would transfer over

1305
00:55:43,570 --> 00:55:44,470
to downstream.

1306
00:55:44,470 --> 00:55:48,530

1307
00:55:48,530 --> 00:55:50,090
Yeah.

1308
00:55:50,090 --> 00:55:52,490
So some of these
are [INAUDIBLE],

1309
00:55:52,490 --> 00:55:54,270
and some are just
[INAUDIBLE] loss.

1310
00:55:54,270 --> 00:55:55,770
When do you do either?

1311
00:55:55,770 --> 00:55:57,330
I see, yes.

1312
00:55:57,330 --> 00:56:00,570
You fit to test loss when you're
doing good scientific practice,

1313
00:56:00,570 --> 00:56:01,910
like Kaplan 2021.

1314
00:56:01,910 --> 00:56:07,450
But realistically, all the
models, except for a very few,

1315
00:56:07,450 --> 00:56:11,430
like the infinite compute
one and the repetition stuff,

1316
00:56:11,430 --> 00:56:15,730
unless you're doing some of
that, you're doing one-pass SGD.

1317
00:56:15,730 --> 00:56:17,850
So the generalization gap
for all of these models

1318
00:56:17,850 --> 00:56:19,130
is very, very small.

1319
00:56:19,130 --> 00:56:21,370
So train and val
are very similar.

1320
00:56:21,370 --> 00:56:23,730
I've seen some pre-training
code bases that don't even

1321
00:56:23,730 --> 00:56:24,990
have validation loss.

1322
00:56:24,990 --> 00:56:27,570
Because if you're doing
one pass, most likely

1323
00:56:27,570 --> 00:56:29,610
your train and val
are very, very close.

1324
00:56:29,610 --> 00:56:32,060
That's why a lot of these
plots exchange those two,

1325
00:56:32,060 --> 00:56:35,343
because they're all in
the one-pass regime.

1326
00:56:35,343 --> 00:56:36,260
Good question, though.

1327
00:56:36,260 --> 00:56:40,920
OK, all right, we will
continue to move on to--

1328
00:56:40,920 --> 00:56:44,100
sorry, I moved on too
far backwards here.

1329
00:56:44,100 --> 00:56:47,680
OK, so now I'm going
to talk about one

1330
00:56:47,680 --> 00:56:51,800
of the most well-known
uses of a scaling law.

1331
00:56:51,800 --> 00:56:55,340
Even though you might not
personally be using this,

1332
00:56:55,340 --> 00:56:58,240
you've certainly used the
rule of thumb at some point.

1333
00:56:58,240 --> 00:57:01,080
And also, I think because
this set of things

1334
00:57:01,080 --> 00:57:04,400
will tell us something really
interesting about the actual

1335
00:57:04,400 --> 00:57:06,680
execution of a scaling law.

1336
00:57:06,680 --> 00:57:09,280
OK, the motivating
question is, do I

1337
00:57:09,280 --> 00:57:11,020
want more data or bigger models?

1338
00:57:11,020 --> 00:57:13,640
So the resource that
you have, the resource

1339
00:57:13,640 --> 00:57:15,160
that you have to
spend, is compute.

1340
00:57:15,160 --> 00:57:17,920
Someone's going to hand you
metaphorically a certain amount

1341
00:57:17,920 --> 00:57:18,620
of FLOPs.

1342
00:57:18,620 --> 00:57:21,180
And you're going to
take those FLOPs,

1343
00:57:21,180 --> 00:57:22,880
and you're going to
spend them somehow

1344
00:57:22,880 --> 00:57:26,320
on-- if you remember Percy's
very first lecture, it's

1345
00:57:26,320 --> 00:57:28,870
going to be data times
the parameters is roughly

1346
00:57:28,870 --> 00:57:30,670
your FLOPs, linear in that.

1347
00:57:30,670 --> 00:57:32,890
So do I want more
data or more models?

1348
00:57:32,890 --> 00:57:36,990
Well, we know that if we
take a teeny tiny model

1349
00:57:36,990 --> 00:57:40,170
and we dump tons of data into
it, that will just be wasted.

1350
00:57:40,170 --> 00:57:43,850
That's the dark purple
line at the very top here.

1351
00:57:43,850 --> 00:57:45,830
I've taken a teeny
tiny language model,

1352
00:57:45,830 --> 00:57:49,010
and I've passed a huge
amount of data into it,

1353
00:57:49,010 --> 00:57:51,330
and this thing has been
flat for a very long time.

1354
00:57:51,330 --> 00:57:53,270
This is a complete
waste of compute.

1355
00:57:53,270 --> 00:57:55,790
You would have much rather
trained this big yellow model

1356
00:57:55,790 --> 00:57:58,030
to the same amount of
tokens or even earlier

1357
00:57:58,030 --> 00:58:01,150
on, if you're having the
same amount of compute.

1358
00:58:01,150 --> 00:58:06,030
Now, if you could understand
the interaction of how

1359
00:58:06,030 --> 00:58:11,270
data amount and model size
come together to give you

1360
00:58:11,270 --> 00:58:13,610
performance, if you
understood that relationship,

1361
00:58:13,610 --> 00:58:16,010
you could optimize
this very precisely.

1362
00:58:16,010 --> 00:58:21,350
And so there are scaling
laws that are more advanced

1363
00:58:21,350 --> 00:58:24,890
and relate kind of joint
behaviors of objects.

1364
00:58:24,890 --> 00:58:30,100
So almost simultaneously,
Kaplan and Rosenfeld

1365
00:58:30,100 --> 00:58:32,920
both proposed these
functional forms,

1366
00:58:32,920 --> 00:58:35,740
which are roughly
equivalent, describing

1367
00:58:35,740 --> 00:58:38,420
how the size of the model
and the amount of data

1368
00:58:38,420 --> 00:58:40,500
relate to the error.

1369
00:58:40,500 --> 00:58:41,840
Rosenfeld is very simple.

1370
00:58:41,840 --> 00:58:44,120
It's really just the sum
of two inverse terms.

1371
00:58:44,120 --> 00:58:46,980
These are two scaling laws
that are added together.

1372
00:58:46,980 --> 00:58:49,480
And Kaplan is a little
bit more complicated,

1373
00:58:49,480 --> 00:58:52,128
but it's roughly the same idea.

1374
00:58:52,128 --> 00:58:53,920
And if you look at,
think about the limits,

1375
00:58:53,920 --> 00:58:54,960
this is very intuitive.

1376
00:58:54,960 --> 00:58:58,660
If I have an infinite amount
of data, then what happens?

1377
00:58:58,660 --> 00:59:00,260
Then this goes to 0.

1378
00:59:00,260 --> 00:59:01,920
You're basically
model-size limited.

1379
00:59:01,920 --> 00:59:03,880
You become a pure
model size scaling law.

1380
00:59:03,880 --> 00:59:06,120
If I send my model
size to infinity,

1381
00:59:06,120 --> 00:59:08,200
then I'm going to
be bounded by data.

1382
00:59:08,200 --> 00:59:10,000
I'm going to have a
pure data scaling law.

1383
00:59:10,000 --> 00:59:12,600
So it makes sense if you take
the limits of both of these.

1384
00:59:12,600 --> 00:59:15,940
It's often a good idea if
someone tells you a scaling law

1385
00:59:15,940 --> 00:59:17,740
to take the limits
of all the variables

1386
00:59:17,740 --> 00:59:21,100
to understand the
behavior of the system.

1387
00:59:21,100 --> 00:59:22,820
Now, the thing
that's kind of cool

1388
00:59:22,820 --> 00:59:25,730
is Rosenfeld and
many others that

1389
00:59:25,730 --> 00:59:28,890
have proposed these kinds
of joint scaling laws

1390
00:59:28,890 --> 00:59:31,870
show that these things fit quite
well, even if you extrapolate.

1391
00:59:31,870 --> 00:59:33,370
So what you're going
to do is you're

1392
00:59:33,370 --> 00:59:36,010
going to fit on these green
points, which is a low compute

1393
00:59:36,010 --> 00:59:37,910
regime and low data regime.

1394
00:59:37,910 --> 00:59:41,730
You never see things that have
either high data or high model

1395
00:59:41,730 --> 00:59:42,430
sizes.

1396
00:59:42,430 --> 00:59:44,570
And then you can
extrapolate on the basis

1397
00:59:44,570 --> 00:59:47,250
of these tiny runs in
this corner over here

1398
00:59:47,250 --> 00:59:51,850
to essentially this high compute
or high data, high model size

1399
00:59:51,850 --> 00:59:52,350
regime.

1400
00:59:52,350 --> 00:59:54,610
And you can very accurately
predict the error

1401
00:59:54,610 --> 00:59:56,210
based on your predictions.

1402
00:59:56,210 --> 00:59:58,690
Great predictions.

1403
00:59:58,690 --> 01:00:01,650
And so once you have that,
now what you can do is to say,

1404
01:00:01,650 --> 01:00:05,050
well, I have a fixed amount
of FLOPs subject to my FLOPs

1405
01:00:05,050 --> 01:00:07,110
constraints, minimize this.

1406
01:00:07,110 --> 01:00:09,830
That's a simple nonlinear
optimization problem.

1407
01:00:09,830 --> 01:00:11,850
You can do that
to solve for this.

1408
01:00:11,850 --> 01:00:19,250
Now, both Kaplan and Rosenfeld
provide equations of this form.

1409
01:00:19,250 --> 01:00:21,790
What do they say?

1410
01:00:21,790 --> 01:00:25,462
Well, if you look at Kaplan, and
they solve for this equation,

1411
01:00:25,462 --> 01:00:26,670
they say something like this.

1412
01:00:26,670 --> 01:00:29,770
Well, the amount of--

1413
01:00:29,770 --> 01:00:30,710
they always flip this.

1414
01:00:30,710 --> 01:00:31,570
It confuses me.

1415
01:00:31,570 --> 01:00:35,950
The amount of data it
should be C to the 0.73,

1416
01:00:35,950 --> 01:00:39,722
and the amount of model size
should be C to the 0.27.

1417
01:00:39,722 --> 01:00:40,430
Is that reversed?

1418
01:00:40,430 --> 01:00:41,230
I think it's reversed, sorry.

1419
01:00:41,230 --> 01:00:42,730
N should be the
amount of parameters

1420
01:00:42,730 --> 01:00:46,010
and D should be
the amount of data.

1421
01:00:46,010 --> 01:00:49,250
And so tokens per
parameters decreases with C.

1422
01:00:49,250 --> 01:00:51,490
So as you increase
the compute amounts,

1423
01:00:51,490 --> 01:00:53,450
this prescription
by Kaplan tells

1424
01:00:53,450 --> 01:00:56,010
you to train bigger and
bigger and bigger models.

1425
01:00:56,010 --> 01:00:58,303
And so if you were around
in the days of GPT-3,

1426
01:00:58,303 --> 01:00:59,970
it kind of shocks me
to think that there

1427
01:00:59,970 --> 01:01:02,890
are people who
were not, you would

1428
01:01:02,890 --> 01:01:05,410
know that there was a
period where everyone was

1429
01:01:05,410 --> 01:01:06,730
training these gigantic models.

1430
01:01:06,730 --> 01:01:09,370
Like hundreds of
billions of parameters

1431
01:01:09,370 --> 01:01:12,770
of models, trillion-parameter
dense models, what have you.

1432
01:01:12,770 --> 01:01:14,570
And part of that
was driven by this.

1433
01:01:14,570 --> 01:01:17,110
They saw GPT-3, they saw
these Kaplan predictions,

1434
01:01:17,110 --> 01:01:19,570
and they said, "Wow, I'm going
to do my big training runs

1435
01:01:19,570 --> 01:01:24,520
on these gigantic
model size models."

1436
01:01:24,520 --> 01:01:29,943
But a few years later, in 2022,
some folks at DeepMind, Hoffman

1437
01:01:29,943 --> 01:01:31,860
et al., came in and they
said, well, actually,

1438
01:01:31,860 --> 01:01:33,793
these predictions
are all terribly off.

1439
01:01:33,793 --> 01:01:35,960
You've been training these
giant models, these three

1440
01:01:35,960 --> 01:01:39,060
stars over here, but actually,
those are way too big.

1441
01:01:39,060 --> 01:01:40,560
What we should be
training instead

1442
01:01:40,560 --> 01:01:45,000
is a very different trend, this
line on the blue over here,

1443
01:01:45,000 --> 01:01:47,440
and we should be training
this teal star, which

1444
01:01:47,440 --> 01:01:48,640
is a much better model.

1445
01:01:48,640 --> 01:01:51,480
So they argued that we should
be training much smaller models

1446
01:01:51,480 --> 01:01:53,160
relative to this.

1447
01:01:53,160 --> 01:01:55,960
And I think, of
course, many of you

1448
01:01:55,960 --> 01:01:57,953
or all of you probably
the Chinchilla paper,

1449
01:01:57,953 --> 01:02:00,160
you know what the
token multipliers are.

1450
01:02:00,160 --> 01:02:02,080
It's 20 tokens to
every parameter.

1451
01:02:02,080 --> 01:02:04,600
But I think this is
useful to go in detail

1452
01:02:04,600 --> 01:02:08,420
because the Chinchilla-Kaplan
disagreement and all of this

1453
01:02:08,420 --> 01:02:10,680
tells us something important
about how we should

1454
01:02:10,680 --> 01:02:11,972
be thinking about scaling laws.

1455
01:02:11,972 --> 01:02:14,013
It's not just something
where you turn the crank,

1456
01:02:14,013 --> 01:02:15,200
and you get the answer.

1457
01:02:15,200 --> 01:02:17,660
It's something that's actually
careful and sensitive,

1458
01:02:17,660 --> 01:02:21,887
and we have to
respect the process.

1459
01:02:21,887 --> 01:02:23,970
So I'm going to walk through
the Chinchilla paper.

1460
01:02:23,970 --> 01:02:25,650
And after I walk through
the Chinchilla paper,

1461
01:02:25,650 --> 01:02:27,910
I'm going to talk about why
the Kaplan and Chinchilla

1462
01:02:27,910 --> 01:02:30,810
papers disagree and
what that means for you.

1463
01:02:30,810 --> 01:02:34,230
So the Chinchilla author
suggests three different ways

1464
01:02:34,230 --> 01:02:35,370
of fitting scaling laws.

1465
01:02:35,370 --> 01:02:39,110
I really like this because it's
a way of robustifying yourself

1466
01:02:39,110 --> 01:02:41,750
to modeling assumptions
you may have made.

1467
01:02:41,750 --> 01:02:44,470
And each of these actually
is quite a different approach

1468
01:02:44,470 --> 01:02:49,710
to estimating the trade-off
between model size and data.

1469
01:02:49,710 --> 01:02:53,550
And minus method 3, which
does differ a little bit,

1470
01:02:53,550 --> 01:02:54,770
they mostly agree.

1471
01:02:54,770 --> 01:02:57,990
All 1 and 2 both agree
that the coefficients

1472
01:02:57,990 --> 01:03:01,310
for data and for model
size are roughly the same.

1473
01:03:01,310 --> 01:03:04,030
0.5, 0.5, 0.49, 0.51.

1474
01:03:04,030 --> 01:03:08,270
Method 3 does differ, 0.46 and
0.54, but we'll talk about why

1475
01:03:08,270 --> 01:03:09,130
later.

1476
01:03:09,130 --> 01:03:11,550
Kaplan et al., of course,
have a very big difference

1477
01:03:11,550 --> 01:03:14,350
between the two, which is why
they were training big models

1478
01:03:14,350 --> 01:03:17,350
at big, big compute sizes.

1479
01:03:17,350 --> 01:03:19,940
Method number 1
from Chinchilla is

1480
01:03:19,940 --> 01:03:22,200
what I might call the
lower envelope method.

1481
01:03:22,200 --> 01:03:25,940
And this is the method that
was also being used partially

1482
01:03:25,940 --> 01:03:29,500
by Kaplan to try to figure out
what the right scaling was.

1483
01:03:29,500 --> 01:03:32,400
And so what you do is you take
all of your training runs.

1484
01:03:32,400 --> 01:03:36,042
So each one of these colored
lines is a training curve.

1485
01:03:36,042 --> 01:03:37,500
And what you want
to do is you want

1486
01:03:37,500 --> 01:03:39,820
to actually take the lower
envelope of these training

1487
01:03:39,820 --> 01:03:40,420
curves.

1488
01:03:40,420 --> 01:03:43,180
Because a lower envelope
point on this training curve,

1489
01:03:43,180 --> 01:03:45,480
that means that for
a particular FLOP,

1490
01:03:45,480 --> 01:03:48,900
this is the best loss that I
ever achieved for that FLOP

1491
01:03:48,900 --> 01:03:50,040
on any training run.

1492
01:03:50,040 --> 01:03:52,620
So I can take all of
the lower envelope

1493
01:03:52,620 --> 01:03:55,640
points at the very bottom here
at the high compute regime,

1494
01:03:55,640 --> 01:03:57,420
and then I can plot
all those points

1495
01:03:57,420 --> 01:04:01,020
to see how big were the models
that each of these FLOP points

1496
01:04:01,020 --> 01:04:01,710
intersected.

1497
01:04:01,710 --> 01:04:03,460
Because each of these
points is a training

1498
01:04:03,460 --> 01:04:05,080
run with a model size.

1499
01:04:05,080 --> 01:04:08,160
And if I scatter plot that, I
will get a nice linear trend.

1500
01:04:08,160 --> 01:04:11,660
And that will give me one way of
estimating the optimal trade-off

1501
01:04:11,660 --> 01:04:13,840
between FLOP to parameters.

1502
01:04:13,840 --> 01:04:16,840
And that gives one answer, which
is for their compute budget,

1503
01:04:16,840 --> 01:04:19,580
they should train a 67
billion parameter model.

1504
01:04:19,580 --> 01:04:23,100
This is a nice and simple way,
but very tricky defining things

1505
01:04:23,100 --> 01:04:25,500
like what is the lower envelope,
and so on and so forth.

1506
01:04:25,500 --> 01:04:28,740
There's definitely
some tricky parts.

1507
01:04:28,740 --> 01:04:30,580
Method 2 is IsoFLOP.

1508
01:04:30,580 --> 01:04:32,200
This is now very popular.

1509
01:04:32,200 --> 01:04:34,160
It's also very easy
and very robust.

1510
01:04:34,160 --> 01:04:37,140
This is probably my
personal favorite method.

1511
01:04:37,140 --> 01:04:39,100
And what you do
is, for this, you

1512
01:04:39,100 --> 01:04:41,600
pick a bunch of
different FLOPS budgets.

1513
01:04:41,600 --> 01:04:45,340
So I have a different range of
FLOPS budgets going in factors

1514
01:04:45,340 --> 01:04:49,300
of 3 in this case or 1, 3, 6.

1515
01:04:49,300 --> 01:04:51,140
And what you do is
for each FLOP budget,

1516
01:04:51,140 --> 01:04:53,220
you're going to essentially
sweep the space.

1517
01:04:53,220 --> 01:04:54,860
So here I have one
variable, which

1518
01:04:54,860 --> 01:04:56,600
is the parameter-to-data
trade-off.

1519
01:04:56,600 --> 01:04:58,500
So for each FLOP
budget, I'm going

1520
01:04:58,500 --> 01:05:02,240
to sweep over the parameter to
data trade-off, fixing my FLOPS.

1521
01:05:02,240 --> 01:05:04,580
So I double my data size,
I half my model size,

1522
01:05:04,580 --> 01:05:05,400
so on and so forth.

1523
01:05:05,400 --> 01:05:08,020
And that will generate a
sequence of training runs

1524
01:05:08,020 --> 01:05:11,040
whose terminal loss is going
to sweep out some of curve.

1525
01:05:11,040 --> 01:05:13,500
So each one of these
colored sets of points

1526
01:05:13,500 --> 01:05:15,240
is a fixed FLOP run.

1527
01:05:15,240 --> 01:05:18,110
Each point is sweeping
across different parameters,

1528
01:05:18,110 --> 01:05:20,910
and of course, it's implicitly
sweeping over data as well.

1529
01:05:20,910 --> 01:05:25,050
Now, the nice thing now is I
can take the minimum over each

1530
01:05:25,050 --> 01:05:26,770
of these runs, or
even fit a quadratic

1531
01:05:26,770 --> 01:05:28,810
and take the minimum
over the quadratics,

1532
01:05:28,810 --> 01:05:30,570
and then I can draw
a line through all

1533
01:05:30,570 --> 01:05:32,230
of the bottoms of
the quadratics,

1534
01:05:32,230 --> 01:05:34,490
or take the bottom
of the quadratics

1535
01:05:34,490 --> 01:05:38,050
and plot them as a function
of FLOPS to parameters.

1536
01:05:38,050 --> 01:05:39,787
And that will give me
another prediction,

1537
01:05:39,787 --> 01:05:40,870
which agrees very closely.

1538
01:05:40,870 --> 01:05:44,190
These is $63 billion parameters
for my compute budget.

1539
01:05:44,190 --> 01:05:47,503

1540
01:05:47,503 --> 01:05:50,930
Now, the final
approach that we can do

1541
01:05:50,930 --> 01:05:52,790
is actually the maybe
most natural one,

1542
01:05:52,790 --> 01:05:56,090
as suggested by the Rosenfeld
paper as well as the Kaplan

1543
01:05:56,090 --> 01:06:00,050
paper, because both
of them are propose

1544
01:06:00,050 --> 01:06:03,770
a hypothesized functional
form for how model

1545
01:06:03,770 --> 01:06:06,050
size and data relate to loss.

1546
01:06:06,050 --> 01:06:09,810
So if you have such a joint
functional form, what you can do

1547
01:06:09,810 --> 01:06:13,450
is you can take a whole bunch of
training runs that you've done,

1548
01:06:13,450 --> 01:06:16,600
and you can fit your
loss landscape, which

1549
01:06:16,600 --> 01:06:18,180
is your hypothesized
functional form,

1550
01:06:18,180 --> 01:06:19,560
and you can just
do curve fitting

1551
01:06:19,560 --> 01:06:21,768
to estimate what the
coefficients are in your scaling

1552
01:06:21,768 --> 01:06:22,268
law.

1553
01:06:22,268 --> 01:06:23,800
This is the most
brute-force way.

1554
01:06:23,800 --> 01:06:25,520
You have a hypothesized
relationship

1555
01:06:25,520 --> 01:06:28,520
between loss, data
size, and model size

1556
01:06:28,520 --> 01:06:31,880
train a bunch of models,
do curve fitting.

1557
01:06:31,880 --> 01:06:34,160
Hopefully, fairly
straightforward--

1558
01:06:34,160 --> 01:06:35,380
how you do that.

1559
01:06:35,380 --> 01:06:37,800
Of course, if you're doing
this method, how you do

1560
01:06:37,800 --> 01:06:39,402
the curve fitting
is very important.

1561
01:06:39,402 --> 01:06:40,860
Now, you're fitting
these surfaces,

1562
01:06:40,860 --> 01:06:43,760
there's many variables,
it's kind of tricky.

1563
01:06:43,760 --> 01:06:47,820
This thing also has some
trickiness, just like method 1.

1564
01:06:47,820 --> 01:06:51,000
So it's not a straightforward
way the same way

1565
01:06:51,000 --> 01:06:53,240
that maybe FLOPS is.

1566
01:06:53,240 --> 01:06:56,820
So now that we've gone
through what Chinchilla does,

1567
01:06:56,820 --> 01:06:59,360
it doesn't seem materially
different than what

1568
01:06:59,360 --> 01:07:00,220
Kaplan was doing.

1569
01:07:00,220 --> 01:07:03,080
It's not like Kaplan was
doing one crazy thing and then

1570
01:07:03,080 --> 01:07:05,800
the Chinchilla authors did
something way smarter and you

1571
01:07:05,800 --> 01:07:08,400
said, "Wow, why didn't we
do that from the start?"

1572
01:07:08,400 --> 01:07:10,520
They both did pretty
reasonable stuff.

1573
01:07:10,520 --> 01:07:12,620
But if we look at their
scaling predictions,

1574
01:07:12,620 --> 01:07:14,030
they are very different.

1575
01:07:14,030 --> 01:07:18,110
And in hindsight, we generally
tend to agree with the fact

1576
01:07:18,110 --> 01:07:21,250
that the Chinchilla authors had
more of the correct scaling.

1577
01:07:21,250 --> 01:07:24,210
If you do your own
Chinchilla-style analysis,

1578
01:07:24,210 --> 01:07:27,750
you will find probably scaling
is much closer to Chinchilla.

1579
01:07:27,750 --> 01:07:30,910
Now, so why is there such
a big difference when

1580
01:07:30,910 --> 01:07:34,390
they're both fitting
these joint scaling laws?

1581
01:07:34,390 --> 01:07:37,350
OK, so this is,
I think, where we

1582
01:07:37,350 --> 01:07:42,210
get into the messy realities
of how scaling laws are made.

1583
01:07:42,210 --> 01:07:45,310
Scaling laws can
really change depending

1584
01:07:45,310 --> 01:07:48,230
on the precise ways in which
you're implementing the scaling

1585
01:07:48,230 --> 01:07:50,890
laws, the hyperparameter
settings that you're using,

1586
01:07:50,890 --> 01:07:53,870
and even what the x-axis is.

1587
01:07:53,870 --> 01:07:55,640
So there's a really nice paper.

1588
01:07:55,640 --> 01:07:56,890
Oh, no, I covered the authors.

1589
01:07:56,890 --> 01:08:02,933
I can paste this in the
lecture Slack later.

1590
01:08:02,933 --> 01:08:05,350
--called "Resolving Discrepancy
to Compute-Optimal Scaling

1591
01:08:05,350 --> 01:08:06,770
of Language Models."

1592
01:08:06,770 --> 01:08:07,770
Yair is the last author.

1593
01:08:07,770 --> 01:08:09,750
He was a former student here.

1594
01:08:09,750 --> 01:08:12,590
And they show--
basically, first they

1595
01:08:12,590 --> 01:08:15,110
replicate the
Kaplan result using

1596
01:08:15,110 --> 01:08:16,689
roughly the Kaplan settings.

1597
01:08:16,689 --> 01:08:18,670
And then they say,
OK, well, let's

1598
01:08:18,670 --> 01:08:20,330
change how we count parameters.

1599
01:08:20,330 --> 01:08:22,013
That shifts the
curve a little bit.

1600
01:08:22,013 --> 01:08:23,430
And then they say,
OK, well, let's

1601
01:08:23,430 --> 01:08:25,290
change how learning
rate warmup is done.

1602
01:08:25,290 --> 01:08:26,870
That shifts the
curve a little bit.

1603
01:08:26,870 --> 01:08:29,090
And then let's tune the
optimizer a little bit more.

1604
01:08:29,090 --> 01:08:31,450
And then they get exactly
the Chinchilla result.

1605
01:08:31,450 --> 01:08:33,350
So their argument
here is that there

1606
01:08:33,350 --> 01:08:38,370
is a sequence of what seems very
minor decisions that in the end,

1607
01:08:38,370 --> 01:08:40,750
gives you this big gap
between the Kaplan scaling

1608
01:08:40,750 --> 01:08:42,850
law and the Chinchilla
scaling law.

1609
01:08:42,850 --> 01:08:46,069
And I'll explain what
each of these steps

1610
01:08:46,069 --> 01:08:49,229
is, not that that's the
most important single thing,

1611
01:08:49,229 --> 01:08:53,069
but also to emphasize to you how
important the details are here.

1612
01:08:53,069 --> 01:08:55,830
So the very first arrow,
where we go from Kaplan

1613
01:08:55,830 --> 01:09:00,010
to the blue line, that is
how we count parameters.

1614
01:09:00,010 --> 01:09:02,375
So remember, in
Kaplan, I said, well,

1615
01:09:02,375 --> 01:09:03,750
they saw this
weird stuff if they

1616
01:09:03,750 --> 01:09:05,270
included embedding parameters.

1617
01:09:05,270 --> 01:09:07,229
So they excluded
embedding parameters.

1618
01:09:07,229 --> 01:09:09,899
That's generally
an OK thing to do.

1619
01:09:09,899 --> 01:09:12,740
But one thing they also did
that really messed them up

1620
01:09:12,740 --> 01:09:16,380
was they also excluded
the last layer parameter

1621
01:09:16,380 --> 01:09:18,200
counts, the softmax ones.

1622
01:09:18,200 --> 01:09:21,220
Because in many models, the
embedding and the last layer

1623
01:09:21,220 --> 01:09:22,580
linear are dual to each other.

1624
01:09:22,580 --> 01:09:24,380
They have the same shapes.

1625
01:09:24,380 --> 01:09:27,720
The inside has vocab
size by hidden dim.

1626
01:09:27,720 --> 01:09:30,502
The output matrix has
hidden dim by vocab size.

1627
01:09:30,502 --> 01:09:31,960
And because they're
the same shape,

1628
01:09:31,960 --> 01:09:34,043
they said, well, let's
just exclude both of those.

1629
01:09:34,043 --> 01:09:36,220
It turns out whether
you include or exclude

1630
01:09:36,220 --> 01:09:40,700
those has a big material impact
on the shape of the scaling law.

1631
01:09:40,700 --> 01:09:44,399
They also found-- this is
much more of an oversight,

1632
01:09:44,399 --> 01:09:48,060
more than a genuine
difference in opinion,

1633
01:09:48,060 --> 01:09:51,420
that a lot of the models that
were being trained in Kaplan

1634
01:09:51,420 --> 01:09:52,359
were very small.

1635
01:09:52,359 --> 01:09:55,100
And in fact, they were so small
that they were not actually

1636
01:09:55,100 --> 01:09:58,080
converging by the time that the
learning rate warm up was done.

1637
01:09:58,080 --> 01:10:00,280
So their learning rates
were set very suboptimally.

1638
01:10:00,280 --> 01:10:03,740
And because of that, their
models weren't fully converged.

1639
01:10:03,740 --> 01:10:07,300
And then finally, they found
that Kaplan et al. was fixing

1640
01:10:07,300 --> 01:10:09,650
one big batch size, and
those big batch sizes

1641
01:10:09,650 --> 01:10:11,670
were suboptimal for
the smaller models.

1642
01:10:11,670 --> 01:10:14,170
And therefore, if you
tune that correctly

1643
01:10:14,170 --> 01:10:15,890
and you use varying
batch sizes, you

1644
01:10:15,890 --> 01:10:20,210
end up getting this thing that
agrees exactly with Chinchilla.

1645
01:10:20,210 --> 01:10:21,890
So I think each of
these might seem

1646
01:10:21,890 --> 01:10:25,912
like a very minor difference,
but if you change up

1647
01:10:25,912 --> 01:10:27,370
these really minor
differences, you

1648
01:10:27,370 --> 01:10:29,810
can get big shifts
in the scaling law.

1649
01:10:29,810 --> 01:10:32,050
And one thing I
like to emphasize

1650
01:10:32,050 --> 01:10:35,530
is that scaling laws are
lower bounds in some sense.

1651
01:10:35,530 --> 01:10:37,770
They're kind of saying,
if I continue this recipe

1652
01:10:37,770 --> 01:10:41,210
and I scale it up, then
this is what I will get.

1653
01:10:41,210 --> 01:10:43,610
But if you're scaling up a
recipe that you don't want

1654
01:10:43,610 --> 01:10:45,508
to scale up, your
warmup is kind of crazy,

1655
01:10:45,508 --> 01:10:47,050
or your batch sizes
are crazy, you're

1656
01:10:47,050 --> 01:10:48,450
going to get bad scaling laws.

1657
01:10:48,450 --> 01:10:54,050
So you want to be as close to
the proper full run as possible.

1658
01:10:54,050 --> 01:10:57,450
There's another paper that does
another very interesting thing

1659
01:10:57,450 --> 01:11:00,130
to try to study the difference
between Kaplan and Chinchilla

1660
01:11:00,130 --> 01:11:01,010
scaling laws.

1661
01:11:01,010 --> 01:11:03,450
It's by Pearce and Song.

1662
01:11:03,450 --> 01:11:06,210
And what they do, and I
think this is very clever,

1663
01:11:06,210 --> 01:11:07,620
is they don't train any models.

1664
01:11:07,620 --> 01:11:09,560
They just take the
Chinchilla training

1665
01:11:09,560 --> 01:11:12,640
curves or the implied functional
form of the Chinchilla training

1666
01:11:12,640 --> 01:11:13,140
curves.

1667
01:11:13,140 --> 01:11:15,500
They just plot them
out and they say, well,

1668
01:11:15,500 --> 01:11:17,560
based on these implied
training curves, which

1669
01:11:17,560 --> 01:11:20,120
are simulated purely
from Chinchilla,

1670
01:11:20,120 --> 01:11:22,400
they generate what the
implied training curves will

1671
01:11:22,400 --> 01:11:25,520
be for Kaplan if you ignore
the embedding parameters

1672
01:11:25,520 --> 01:11:27,920
and you scale way
down, and what happens

1673
01:11:27,920 --> 01:11:30,300
if you use the total
parameter count instead.

1674
01:11:30,300 --> 01:11:32,580
And what they find is
really two effects.

1675
01:11:32,580 --> 01:11:35,900
One is that Kaplan is operating
at a much lower compute scale,

1676
01:11:35,900 --> 01:11:38,260
so they're very sensitive
to small changes,

1677
01:11:38,260 --> 01:11:40,600
and actually ignoring the
non-embedding parameter

1678
01:11:40,600 --> 01:11:43,720
is a sufficiently big change
to mess up the result relative

1679
01:11:43,720 --> 01:11:44,500
to Chinchilla.

1680
01:11:44,500 --> 01:11:48,060
So their argument is slightly
different from Yair and others,

1681
01:11:48,060 --> 01:11:49,520
which is that here,
actually, it's

1682
01:11:49,520 --> 01:11:53,160
the size of the compute scale
plus the slight non-linearity

1683
01:11:53,160 --> 01:11:55,360
induced by non embedding
parameters that's

1684
01:11:55,360 --> 01:11:57,700
causing this disagreement.

1685
01:11:57,700 --> 01:12:01,580
So this is really starting
to get into the weeds.

1686
01:12:01,580 --> 01:12:04,520
But I hope you
appreciate the importance

1687
01:12:04,520 --> 01:12:06,420
of paying attention to detail.

1688
01:12:06,420 --> 01:12:09,080
And I think a lot of
the work that goes on

1689
01:12:09,080 --> 01:12:11,120
in establishing,
relying, scaling laws

1690
01:12:11,120 --> 01:12:13,320
is about figuring out
what the right regime is

1691
01:12:13,320 --> 01:12:17,880
so that you can get reliable
scaling across compute scales.

1692
01:12:17,880 --> 01:12:20,680
The final thing, and this is
really just more of something

1693
01:12:20,680 --> 01:12:23,760
that I just find amusing more
than anything else, which

1694
01:12:23,760 --> 01:12:26,240
is that Chinchilla is
this paper that takes down

1695
01:12:26,240 --> 01:12:27,380
the Kaplan paper in a way.

1696
01:12:27,380 --> 01:12:29,720
It's like, oh, you
guys were very wrong.

1697
01:12:29,720 --> 01:12:32,020
But there was this
one remaining mystery,

1698
01:12:32,020 --> 01:12:34,560
which is that Chinchilla
method three never agreed

1699
01:12:34,560 --> 01:12:36,140
with Chinchilla method 1 and 2.

1700
01:12:36,140 --> 01:12:38,140
And the numbers look small.

1701
01:12:38,140 --> 01:12:40,535
And the authors are very
unperturbed about this,

1702
01:12:40,535 --> 01:12:42,660
but I think the implications
are quite non-trivial.

1703
01:12:42,660 --> 01:12:45,280
If you look at this,
methods 1 and 2 basically

1704
01:12:45,280 --> 01:12:48,498
say the amount of parameters and
tokens should scale together.

1705
01:12:48,498 --> 01:12:50,540
So there should be a fixed
ratio between the two.

1706
01:12:50,540 --> 01:12:53,200
This is where the Chinchilla
factor of 20 comes from.

1707
01:12:53,200 --> 01:12:56,660
If you look at number 3,
the exponents are different.

1708
01:12:56,660 --> 01:12:58,600
This means if you
scale up your compute,

1709
01:12:58,600 --> 01:13:00,220
eventually, actually,
in this case,

1710
01:13:00,220 --> 01:13:02,160
you're going to have
way more tokens than you

1711
01:13:02,160 --> 01:13:03,542
are going to have parameters.

1712
01:13:03,542 --> 01:13:05,750
So it's going to be actually
a very different scaling

1713
01:13:05,750 --> 01:13:09,910
conclusion asymptotically
than the other ones.

1714
01:13:09,910 --> 01:13:15,282
And so it turns out that I was
certainly not the only one who

1715
01:13:15,282 --> 01:13:18,310
was perturbed about this,
except some folks at Epoch AI

1716
01:13:18,310 --> 01:13:20,470
did something about
being perturbed.

1717
01:13:20,470 --> 01:13:23,210
They couldn't get either
the raw data or the code.

1718
01:13:23,210 --> 01:13:25,510
So what they did was they
extracted the data from plots

1719
01:13:25,510 --> 01:13:29,545
in the paper, and they refitted
the method 3 regressions.

1720
01:13:29,545 --> 01:13:31,170
They extracted the
data from the paper.

1721
01:13:31,170 --> 01:13:35,990
And given that data, they fitted
that 2D surface or 3D surface

1722
01:13:35,990 --> 01:13:37,710
that was proposed.

1723
01:13:37,710 --> 01:13:39,590
And then, what they
showed is actually

1724
01:13:39,590 --> 01:13:43,570
the original paper must have
underfitted the original data.

1725
01:13:43,570 --> 01:13:46,170
And if they redid
the curve fitting,

1726
01:13:46,170 --> 01:13:48,910
they could get much lower
losses relative to what

1727
01:13:48,910 --> 01:13:52,250
the proposed scaling law
in the Chinchilla paper.

1728
01:13:52,250 --> 01:13:55,650
And in fact, if you
follow the refitted model,

1729
01:13:55,650 --> 01:13:59,870
you got almost
exactly D over 20 is

1730
01:13:59,870 --> 01:14:02,220
20 rule of thumb from
the Chinchilla paper,

1731
01:14:02,220 --> 01:14:03,840
which is really a
funny conclusion,

1732
01:14:03,840 --> 01:14:05,980
I think, to the Chinchilla
paper, where in the end

1733
01:14:05,980 --> 01:14:09,540
it turns out that the authors
were more right than they knew.

1734
01:14:09,540 --> 01:14:11,540
Actually, they had
made a mistake,

1735
01:14:11,540 --> 01:14:13,780
and that was the only reason
why method 3 disagreed

1736
01:14:13,780 --> 01:14:14,840
from methods 1 and 2.

1737
01:14:14,840 --> 01:14:19,220
So it's a nice conclusion
to know that the method was

1738
01:14:19,220 --> 01:14:21,580
robust after all.

1739
01:14:21,580 --> 01:14:25,600
OK, so that's the end of the
Chinchilla saga in some ways.

1740
01:14:25,600 --> 01:14:29,740
But I will end this section
by saying you probably don't

1741
01:14:29,740 --> 01:14:31,340
want the Chinchilla factor.

1742
01:14:31,340 --> 01:14:35,220
At this point, I think this is
a pretty common knowledge thing.

1743
01:14:35,220 --> 01:14:37,420
But if you're training
a production model,

1744
01:14:37,420 --> 01:14:40,260
you don't really care about
saving training compute

1745
01:14:40,260 --> 01:14:41,540
for the most part.

1746
01:14:41,540 --> 01:14:43,460
You can look at all
sorts of external surveys

1747
01:14:43,460 --> 01:14:45,660
that people have done
of how Frontier Labs are

1748
01:14:45,660 --> 01:14:46,960
spending their compute.

1749
01:14:46,960 --> 01:14:49,400
Most of the compute is not
going into training runs.

1750
01:14:49,400 --> 01:14:53,480
Most of the compute is actually
going into R&D and serving.

1751
01:14:53,480 --> 01:14:56,540
And given that, especially for
serving, what you want to do

1752
01:14:56,540 --> 01:14:59,100
is you want small
models that are capable.

1753
01:14:59,100 --> 01:15:02,790
You do not want big, bloated
models that cost a lot to serve,

1754
01:15:02,790 --> 01:15:05,050
even if that minimizes
training cost.

1755
01:15:05,050 --> 01:15:08,810
And so what you really want is
something that you would call

1756
01:15:08,810 --> 01:15:09,730
as "overtrained."

1757
01:15:09,730 --> 01:15:12,003
I put that in quotes
because, really, overtraining

1758
01:15:12,003 --> 01:15:12,670
is what we want.

1759
01:15:12,670 --> 01:15:14,410
That's the right
amount of training.

1760
01:15:14,410 --> 01:15:16,970
And if you look at all the
models that were released

1761
01:15:16,970 --> 01:15:18,790
in the early days, like GPT-3--

1762
01:15:18,790 --> 01:15:21,830
this was under-trained for sure,
three tokens per parameter.

1763
01:15:21,830 --> 01:15:25,030
Chinchilla gets to the modern
ratio of 20 per parameter.

1764
01:15:25,030 --> 01:15:27,430
And then for a while, people
were at 20 per parameter.

1765
01:15:27,430 --> 01:15:28,970
And this is the
era, I think, where

1766
01:15:28,970 --> 01:15:31,070
there wasn't that much
serving of these models.

1767
01:15:31,070 --> 01:15:34,110
The models were cool, but it
wasn't being served at scale.

1768
01:15:34,110 --> 01:15:38,170
And then as you start to move to
eras where serving becomes very

1769
01:15:38,170 --> 01:15:39,970
real, now you're
overtraining these models,

1770
01:15:39,970 --> 01:15:41,490
and now you've moved
into MoEs, and you're

1771
01:15:41,490 --> 01:15:43,990
making all these trade-offs
to optimize inference serving.

1772
01:15:43,990 --> 01:15:47,070
So I think Chinchilla
is important,

1773
01:15:47,070 --> 01:15:51,310
not because I think the 20-to-1
ratio is the one golden ratio.

1774
01:15:51,310 --> 01:15:54,022
If you're doing stuff for
research, it might be.

1775
01:15:54,022 --> 01:15:55,730
But I think the reason
why it's important

1776
01:15:55,730 --> 01:15:58,010
is because it tells us
quite a bit about how

1777
01:15:58,010 --> 01:15:59,770
we should be
fitting scaling laws

1778
01:15:59,770 --> 01:16:02,090
and how to think
about scaling laws.

1779
01:16:02,090 --> 01:16:05,770
OK, one final thing I'll
end with Chinchilla-wise

1780
01:16:05,770 --> 01:16:08,100
and otherwise for
scaling is IsoFLOP

1781
01:16:08,100 --> 01:16:09,850
have been one of the
things that, I think,

1782
01:16:09,850 --> 01:16:12,290
has a research
tool have endured.

1783
01:16:12,290 --> 01:16:14,290
IsoFLOP are very
easy to execute.

1784
01:16:14,290 --> 01:16:16,010
You fix a FLOPS
budget, and then you

1785
01:16:16,010 --> 01:16:18,330
sweep over all of the
other degrees of freedom

1786
01:16:18,330 --> 01:16:20,450
to see what the
surface looks like.

1787
01:16:20,450 --> 01:16:24,610
And this gives you, in general,
fairly reliable readings

1788
01:16:24,610 --> 01:16:27,850
of how different parameters
vary as a function

1789
01:16:27,850 --> 01:16:31,250
of your free parameters.

1790
01:16:31,250 --> 01:16:34,050
People have done this for-- or
actually, one of my students

1791
01:16:34,050 --> 01:16:35,910
did this for diffusion models.

1792
01:16:35,910 --> 01:16:39,770
The MoE study I talked about
was a IsoFLOP-style design.

1793
01:16:39,770 --> 01:16:42,030
If you're ever in a situation
where you're thinking,

1794
01:16:42,030 --> 01:16:44,830
how am I going to decide
all these trade-offs,

1795
01:16:44,830 --> 01:16:47,970
IsoFLOP is always
a good default.

1796
01:16:47,970 --> 01:16:52,810
OK, so to wrap up
everything for today,

1797
01:16:52,810 --> 01:16:56,490
there's this idea that we have
this log-linear regularity

1798
01:16:56,490 --> 01:16:58,800
between the amount of
resources we put in

1799
01:16:58,800 --> 01:17:00,140
and the performance we get out.

1800
01:17:00,140 --> 01:17:02,840
And it extends to a wide variety
of things, model parameters,

1801
01:17:02,840 --> 01:17:05,880
compute, sparsity
level of your MoE.

1802
01:17:05,880 --> 01:17:07,800
And that lets you do
a lot of the things

1803
01:17:07,800 --> 01:17:10,740
that I was talking about
earlier, as arbitrary choices,

1804
01:17:10,740 --> 01:17:14,400
but do it in a much more
evidence-driven way, hopefully.

1805
01:17:14,400 --> 01:17:17,640
And so this allows us to
have engineering at scale

1806
01:17:17,640 --> 01:17:22,340
without explicitly relying on
doing big model training runs.

1807
01:17:22,340 --> 01:17:24,650
And hopefully, you
remember, I think all

1808
01:17:24,650 --> 01:17:25,900
the different components here.

1809
01:17:25,900 --> 01:17:28,100
Data scaling is this
very natural object,

1810
01:17:28,100 --> 01:17:29,920
just the exponent is
a little bit strange.

1811
01:17:29,920 --> 01:17:32,440
Model scaling allows you to
do all these cool engineering

1812
01:17:32,440 --> 01:17:33,020
things.

1813
01:17:33,020 --> 01:17:35,680
And then finally, scaling
is this very cool,

1814
01:17:35,680 --> 01:17:37,960
robust predictor,
let's call it, for how

1815
01:17:37,960 --> 01:17:41,160
to try to do engineering
at a large scale.

1816
01:17:41,160 --> 01:17:41,780
And thanks.

1817
